DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Reflection (All Disciplines)
DESCRIPTION:Reflection is a Pro Helvetia grant supporting collective mediat
 ion projects that use innovative formats to foster critical reflection on 
 artistic practice and cultural transformation\, across all supported disci
 plines (design\, literature\, music\, performing arts\, visual arts\, and 
 innovation and society). Open to collectives comprising at least two cultu
 ral practitioners (artists\, creatives\, cultural practitioners\, or art e
 ducators)\; solo projects are excluded. ELIGIBILITY: requires a connection
  to Switzerland (Swiss citizenship\, or permanent residency with regular a
 rtistic activity in Switzerland)\; not open to practitioners without a Swi
 ss connection. For international collaborations\, at least 50 percent of p
 ractitioners must be from Switzerland. AMOUNT: maximum CHF 25\,000\, and n
 o more than 50 percent of the total budget. Deadlines twice a year: 1 Marc
 h (23:59 CET) and 1 September (23:59 CEST). Apply via the Pro Helvetia por
 tal.\n\nOrganisation: Pro Helvetia (Swiss Arts Council)\nLocation: Collect
 ives of at least two cultural practitioners\; for international collaborat
 ions at least 50 percent must be from Switzerland.\nAward: Maximum CHF 25\
 ,000 (cannot exceed 50 percent of total budget). No application fee.\nDura
 tion: Collective mediation project fostering critical reflection\; deadlin
 es twice yearly (1 March and 1 September).\n\nMore: https://prohelvetia.ch
 /en/find-support/reflection?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://prohelvetia.ch/en/find-support/reflection?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Collectives of at least two cultural practitioners\; for internati
 onal collaborations at least 50 percent must be from Switzerland.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:hibrides-biennial-artistic-research-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Híbrides: Small Embodied Data - Call for Contributions (
 2nd Edition)
DESCRIPTION:Híbrides is a biennial of artistic research co-organised by th
 e Axolot.cat collective and the DARTS (Design\, Art\, Technology and Socie
 ty) research group at the UOC in Barcelona. The second edition\, "Small Em
 bodied Data\," centres on the concept of classification -- approached thro
 ugh machine learning\, data science\, algorithmic systems\, and questions 
 of who designs classifiers\, under what ethical codes\, and to what end. T
 he biennial brings classification into dialogue with its previous themes o
 f Small Data (community-defined\, intentional data collection and analysis
 ) and Embodiment (bodily experience as a foundation of situated\, sensory 
 knowledge). Both academic and non-academic contributions are welcome. FORM
 ATS ACCEPTED: Oral Presentations (20 minutes\; artistic practice\, artisti
 c research\, or academic research projects\; may be video-recorded for a p
 ublic archive and developed into short papers)\; Performances (20-30 minut
 es\; must engage with the physical body\, stage\, and technology\; complet
 e works\, including previously presented work\; takes place in a multipurp
 ose space with technical support). SUBMISSION REQUIREMENTS: online form wi
 th abstract (max 1\,000 characters)\, professional biography (max 1\,000 c
 haracters)\, and link to representative materials (3-5 images\; video and 
 technical rider required for performances). HONORARIA: oral presentations 
 €100\; performances €400\; invoice required in both cases. Participant
 s must follow the event's code of conduct. Event runs 26-28 November 2026 
 at UOC Poblenou campus and Convent de Sant Agustí\, Barcelona. Deadline: 
 7 September 2026. Submit at https://hibrides.axolot.cat/opencall.html.\n\n
 Organisation: Axolot.cat collective and DARTS research group\, Universitat
  Oberta de Catalunya (UOC)\nLocation: Barcelona\, Spain\; UOC Poblenou cam
 pus and Convent de Sant Agustí. In-person attendance required.\nAward: Or
 al presentations: €100 honorarium (invoice required). Performances: €4
 00 honorarium (invoice required). No submission fee.\nDuration: Festival r
 uns 26-28 November 2026.\n\nMore: https://hibrides.axolot.cat/opencall.htm
 l?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://hibrides.axolot.cat/opencall.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Barcelona\, Spain\; UOC Poblenou campus and Convent de Sant Agust
 í. In-person attendance required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:horizon-europe-cl2-2026-heritage-ai-creative@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260923
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260924
SUMMARY:Deadline: Horizon Europe Cluster 2: Culture\, Creativity and Inclus
 ive Society (HORIZON-CL2-2026-01-HERITAGE) — incl. AI and Creative Conte
 nt topic
DESCRIPTION:Horizon Europe Cluster 2 funds research on culture\, creativity
 \, and inclusive society. The 2026 call (HORIZON-CL2-2026-01-HERITAGE\, la
 unched May 12\, 2026) covers 7 topics with an indicative budget of €85.5
  million. Topic HERITAGE-04 is explicitly titled 'Towards a fair and trans
 parent market for cultural and creative content in the era of generative A
 I\,' covering: impacts of generative AI on creative industries employment\
 ; upskilling needs for creative workers\; policy and regulatory frameworks
 \; fair remuneration and transparency. Highly relevant for research into A
 I and cultural/creative industries\, artists' rights in the AI era\, and s
 ociotechnical studies of AI and culture. ELIGIBILITY: consortium-based app
 lications (minimum 3 organisations from 3 different EU/associated countrie
 s are typical for Horizon RIA grants\; check call conditions on the Fundin
 g & Tenders portal for specific requirements). Deadline: 23 September 2026
 . Apply via the EU Funding & Tenders portal (ec.europa.eu/info/funding-ten
 ders).\n\nOrganisation: European Research Executive Agency (REA) / Europea
 n Commission\, Horizon Europe\nLocation: European Union and associated cou
 ntries. Consortium-based\; typically requires partners from multiple EU/Ho
 rizon member states.\nAward: Indicative total budget €85.5 million acros
 s 7 topics (2026 call). Individual project amounts vary by topic. Up to 10
 0% funding rate for non-profit research organizations. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Typically 3-4 years\; varies by topic.\n\nMore: https://rea.ec
 .europa.eu/funding-and-grants/horizon-europe-cluster-2-culture-creativity-
 and-inclusive-society/european-cultural-heritage-and-cultural-and-creative
 -industries_en?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://rea.ec.europa.eu/funding-and-grants/horizon-europe-cluster-2-cu
 lture-creativity-and-inclusive-society/european-cultural-heritage-and-cult
 ural-and-creative-industries_en?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
LOCATION:European Union and associated countries. Consortium-based\; typica
 lly requires partners from multiple EU/Horizon member states.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:european-commission-born-digital-heritage-2026@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260716
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260717
SUMMARY:Deadline: European Commission: Safeguarding Europe's Born-Digital H
 eritage (PPPA-2026-BORN-DIGITAL)
DESCRIPTION:The European Commission is offering a single grant of up to €
 1\,985\,000 (85% co-financing) for a project focused on safeguarding born-
 digital cultural heritage across EU member states. Scope includes: assessi
 ng the preservation state of born-digital art\, web archives\, video games
 \, and digital content created outside cultural institutions\; mapping EU 
 and national legal frameworks\; identifying non-legislative barriers (tech
 nical\, financial\, organisational\, cultural)\; and providing guidance on
  creating preservation copies of licensed or legally accessible materials.
  This is a scoping and advocacy project\, not a digitisation grant. ELIGIB
 ILITY: the application must be led by a consortium of at least 5 independe
 nt entities from 5 different EU countries\, which rules out solo applicant
 s and single-country organisations. Grant reference: PPPA-2026-BORN-DIGITA
 L. Deadline: 16 July 2026. Verify the current deadline at ec.europa.eu bef
 ore applying (one secondary source listed 1 June 2026\; the primary call-f
 iche PDF and the Cyprus government funding portal both confirm 16 July 202
 6).\n\nOrganisation: European Commission (Pilot Projects and Preparatory A
 ctions\, Culture strand)\nLocation: European Union. Consortium of at least
  5 independent entities from 5 different EU member states required.\nAward
 : Up to €1\,985\,000\, covering up to 85% of eligible project costs (15%
  co-financing required). No application fee.\nDuration: Project duration t
 o be defined in the proposal\; covers a scoping and mapping phase across E
 U member states.\n\nMore: https://ec.europa.eu/info/funding-tenders/opport
 unities/docs/2021-2027/pppa/wp-call/2026/call-fiche_pppa-2026-born-digital
 _en.pdf?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://ec.europa.eu/info/funding-tenders/opportunities/docs/2021-2027/
 pppa/wp-call/2026/call-fiche_pppa-2026-born-digital_en.pdf?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:European Union. Consortium of at least 5 independent entities from
  5 different EU member states required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kb-researcher-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260927
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260928
SUMMARY:Deadline: KB Researcher-in-Residence (RiR) 2027
DESCRIPTION:ELIGIBILITY: two Researcher-in-Residence positions in 2027. Ope
 n to early-career researchers of any discipline (data science\, social sci
 ence\, computer science\, humanities) who are currently working on a PhD o
 r within 5 years of their PhD end date. You must have\, or be able to secu
 re\, an affiliation with a Dutch university or research institute willing 
 to second you\; external PhD students may use the funding to obtain such a
  contract. Your project must make use of one or more of the KB digital col
 lections and you must be able to work with primarily Dutch data. You must 
 be available at 0.5 FTE over 6 months and able to spend two days a week on
 -site at the KB in The Hague. COLLECTIONS: over 130 million digitized page
 s of newspapers\, magazines\, radio bulletins and books from 1470 onward (
 via Delpher and DBNL)\, a born-digital web collection of more than 23\,000
  websites\, ground-truth and derived datasets on the KB Lab\, and metadata
  including Linked Data. APPLY: use the official proposal template and subm
 it a single PDF by 27 September 2026 to dh@kb.nl\, after reading the call'
 s terms and conditions\; questions to dh@kb.nl. TIMELINE: deadline 27 Sept
 ember 2026\; internal eligibility review followed by an external committee
  review\; outcome notified November 2026\; residencies start Q1 and Q3 202
 7. DISCIPLINES: digital humanities / data science / computational research
  with cultural-heritage collections. NOTE: the output is usually a dataset
  or tool prototype hosted on the KB Lab\; you agree to share results via b
 log posts on the KB Lab and to publish resulting tools or data under an op
 en license within 6 months of the residency. Proposals should align with t
 he KB Research Agenda or the Cultural AI Lab.\n\nOrganisation: KB\, Nation
 al Library of the Netherlands\nLocation: The Hague\, Netherlands (two days
  per week on-site at the KB premises)\nAward: Secondment at 0.5 FTE for 6 
 months based on your current salary\, up to EUR 5\,590 gross per month for
  a full-time-equivalent position\, plus travel expenses\, office facilitie
 s\, and support from a research software engineer\, research advisor\, and
  collection and data specialists. No application fee.\nDuration: 6 months 
 at 0.5 FTE (two days per week on-site)\; two positions in 2027\, starting 
 Q1 and Q3\n\nMore: https://lab.kb.nl/news/call-kb-researcher-residence-rir
 -2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://lab.kb.nl/news/call-kb-researcher-residence-rir-2027?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:The Hague\, Netherlands (two days per week on-site at the KB premi
 ses)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:effea-call-5-get-ready-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261103
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261104
SUMMARY:Deadline: EFFEA Call #5: European Festivals Fund for Emerging Artis
 ts
DESCRIPTION:The European Festivals Fund for Emerging Artists (EFFEA)\, an i
 nitiative of the European Festivals Association co-funded by the European 
 Union\, will open its fifth call from 1 September to 3 November 2026\, wit
 h full guidelines and application details published on 1 September 2026. C
 all #5 will again support around 50 cross-border co-production residencies
  taking place in 2027\, each bringing together at least three festivals fr
 om different eligible countries to accompany an emerging artist or collect
 ive. EFFEA is a co-production residency scheme: festivals join forces acro
 ss borders to support an emerging artist through a process of creation and
  presentation\, providing dedicated time\, space\, and tailored mentoring\
 , exchange\, and collaboration\, moving beyond a single invitation toward 
 a shared artistic development journey. Each supported project is built aro
 und one leading festival working with at least two partner festivals based
  in different eligible countries\; one emerging artist or collective at th
 e centre\; a combination of artistic development\, mentoring\, exchange\, 
 and audience engagement based on the artist's needs\; and presentations or
  outcomes shared across the partner festivals. Two residency formats are o
 ffered. DISCOVERY (EUR 8\,000 grant): the proposal must include a workshop
  (live or online) hosted by the leading festival with at least one program
 mer from each partner festival\, and a work-in-progress presentation at th
 e leading festival. SPRINGBOARD (EUR 15\,000 grant): the proposal must inc
 lude final presentations or production results shown within each festival 
 (minimum the leading festival plus two partners) and a live masterclass le
 d by the artist(s) at each festival (minimum the leading festival plus two
  partners). WHO CAN APPLY: festivals that have existed for at least one ed
 ition and are registered on FestivalFinder.eu\; the festivals apply on beh
 alf of the residency project. Residency projects must fall within these ar
 tistic fields: architecture\, ballet\, contemporary dance\, circus\, desig
 n/applied arts\, digital arts\, film\, folklore and folk arts\, heritage\,
  interdisciplinary\, literature\, music\, opera\, street art\, theatre\, p
 hotography/video\, puppetry\, sculpture\, painting. A series of EFFEA info
 \, networking\, and Q&A sessions runs on Tuesdays 11:00-12:00 CEST (15 Sep
 tember\, 29 September\, 13 October\, and 20 October 2026) to help applican
 ts navigate the process and find partners. Across Calls #1 to #4\, EFFEA h
 as supported 619 festivals and 200 artists/collectives across Europe and b
 eyond.\n\nOrganisation: European Festivals Association (EFA)\nLocation: Ac
 ross eligible European countries\; residencies built around partner festiv
 als registered on FestivalFinder.eu\nAward: Per-residency grants: EUR 8\,0
 00 (Discovery format) or EUR 15\,000 (Springboard format)\nDuration: Resid
 encies take place in 2027\; call open 1 September to 3 November 2026\, ful
 l guidelines published 1 September 2026\n\nMore: https://www.effea.eu/news
 /item/2026/06/24/effea-call-5-get-ready?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.effea.eu/news/item/2026/06/24/effea-call-5-get-ready?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Across eligible European countries\; residencies built around part
 ner festivals registered on FestivalFinder.eu
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stiftung-kuenstlerdorf-schoeppingen-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Stiftung Künstler*dorf Schöppingen: Residency Fellowshi
 p 2027 Open Call
DESCRIPTION:Stiftung Künstler*dorf Schöppingen offers residency fellowshi
 ps for professional visual artists\, authors and composers\; since 2023 ap
 plications from collectives are also accepted. The call is open to people 
 of all nationalities and ages\, and accommodation for partners\, family me
 mbers or assistance can be arranged upon agreement and availability (for p
 hysical\, cognitive or sensory disabilities\, contact the foundation to ex
 plain any barriers). Fellows reside and work at the Künstler*dorf for a p
 eriod of their choosing between two and six months\, using the premises an
 d infrastructure on-site. The stipend is EUR 1\,500 per month\, minus roug
 hly EUR 100-200 for rent and operating expenses\; for collectives it is EU
 R 1\,000 per person up to a maximum of EUR 3\,000 per month\, minus rent a
 nd operating costs. Travel\, insurance or material costs are covered only 
 in exceptional cases on request. The fellowship is not designed as a produ
 ction residency and there is no obligation to present results\, though exh
 ibition and presentation facilities are available and the voluntary co-org
 anization of events (lectures\, readings\, workshops or similar) is welcom
 ed. A willingness to participate in events and to engage in exchange with 
 other fellows is a prerequisite. Selection is made by expert juries on the
  quality of previous work\, the quality and plausibility of the project fo
 r Schöppingen\, and program contributions (interaction / community buildi
 ng). Applications for 2027 can be submitted from 27 June to 31 August 2026
  (midnight CET) via the online application portal.\n\nOrganisation: Stiftu
 ng Künstler*dorf Schöppingen\nLocation: Schöppingen\, Germany (residenc
 y requires living and working on-site)\nAward: Stipend of EUR 1\,500 per m
 onth\, minus roughly EUR 100-200 for rent and operating expenses. For coll
 ectives\, EUR 1\,000 per person up to a maximum of EUR 3\,000 per month\, 
 minus rent and operating costs. Travel\, insurance and material costs are 
 covered only in exceptional cases\, on request.\nDuration: Residency of tw
 o to six months during 2027\; period chosen by agreement and availability\
 n\nMore: https://stiftung-kuenstlerdorf.de/en/application_process?utm_sour
 ce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://stiftung-kuenstlerdorf.de/en/application_process?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Schöppingen\, Germany (residency requires living and working on-s
 ite)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:phenomenon-grant-kerenidis-pepe-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Phenomenon Grant 2026 (Second Edition)
DESCRIPTION:Created in 2025\, the Phenomenon Grant is a yearly program that
  supports art projects in Greece with grants of EUR 5\,000 to EUR 10\,000 
 for individuals\, collectives\, and non-profit organizations engaged with 
 queer\, feminist\, anti-colonial\, and other emancipatory art practices. T
 he grant aims to support critical phases of artistic development\, enablin
 g experimentation\, research\, collaboration\, and the realization of new 
 and ongoing projects. In a context marked by rising authoritarianism\, gen
 ocidal forces and the dismantling of social justice advancements\, the pro
 gram seeks to support voices that critically and ethically engage with the
 se issues\, create spaces of resistance\, reimagine futures beyond exclusi
 on\, persecution\, and domination\, and open pathways toward collective li
 beration. Particular attention is paid to collaborative endeavours rooted 
 in Greece that are built on connections with the Global Majority\, promoti
 ng networks of translocal solidarity and interconnected struggles. Organiz
 ed and supported by Kerenidis Pepe\, which has pledged a total budget of E
 UR 60\,000 over 2025-2027\, the amount awarded is based on the application
 's actual funding needs. In 2025 the program received 193 applications and
  awarded three grants of EUR 8\,500 each to Athens Museum of Queer Arts (A
 MOQA)\, Forensic Architecture Initiative Athens (FAIA) and Theo Panagopoul
 os. The 2026 selection committee is composed of Athena Athanasiou\, Dora G
 arcia\, Christina Li\, VASKOS\, and chaired by Aggelika Mitsiou. The submi
 ssion period runs from 1 September to 30 September 2026\, with selected pr
 ojects announced in November 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Phenomenon Association
 \, supported by Kerenidis Pepe\nLocation: Greece (projects rooted in Greec
 e)\nAward: Grants ranging from EUR 5\,000 to EUR 10\,000\, based on the ap
 plication's actual funding needs. Each edition awards a minimum of EUR 20\
 ,000 to two to four grantees (in 2025\, three grants of EUR 8\,500 each we
 re awarded).\nDuration: One- or two-year initiatives\; selected projects a
 nnounced November 2026\n\nMore: https://kerenidispepe.art/projects/grants/
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://kerenidispepe.art/projects/grants/?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Greece (projects rooted in Greece)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pee-workshop-matter-out-of-place-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: Open Call: Pee Workshop (Matter Out of Place)
DESCRIPTION:Pee Workshop is looking for 16 performers to take part in a two
 -day research practice led by Nica Roses and Eris Kyriakides as part of th
 e dance and performance event Matter Out of Place. The workshop uses drink
 ing\, holding\, and releasing liquid as a starting point for choreographic
  research\, paying attention to the body's internal timing: what we consum
 e\, how liquid moves through the body\, what the bladder signals\, and how
  the organs produce movements that often go unnoticed. This cycle becomes 
 the structure of the research\, opening into dance\, somatic exercises\, c
 horeographic tasks\, group compositions\, and experiments with collective 
 energy. The material develops through urination but also through the share
 d experience of moving\, waiting\, responding\, and performing together. P
 erformers\, dancers\, movement practitioners\, and artists interested in s
 omatic research\, experimental choreography\, and collective performance-m
 aking are welcome\; professional dance training is not required\, but appl
 icants should feel comfortable working physically and collaboratively. All
  exercises are approached through clear consent\, respect for personal bou
 ndaries\, and collective care. The workshop takes place Monday 17 and Tues
 day 18 August 2026 from 10:00 to 17:00 at The Dirty Art Foundation\, culmi
 nating in an optional 60-minute public showing on Wednesday 19 August. Eac
 h performer receives a fee of EUR 200. To apply\, send a short introductio
 n about yourself\, your movement or performance experience\, and why you a
 re interested in the workshop to nicaxroses@gmail.com by 10 August 2026.\n
 \nOrganisation: Nica Roses & Eris Kyriakides\, at The Dirty Art Foundation
 \nLocation: The Dirty Art Foundation (Amsterdam\, Netherlands)\nAward: Per
 former fee of EUR 200. Participation in the optional public showing is not
  required to receive the fee.\nDuration: Two-day workshop\, Monday 17 and 
 Tuesday 18 August 2026\, 10:00-17:00\, with an optional 60-minute public s
 howing on Wednesday 19 August\n\nMore: https://www.instagram.com/p/DacdGWT
 jTSL/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.instagram.com/p/DacdGWTjTSL/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:The Dirty Art Foundation (Amsterdam\, Netherlands)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-publication-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260730
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260731
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: Publication
DESCRIPTION:The Mondriaan Fund's Publication grant supports the production 
 of special publications about living visual artists from the Netherlands o
 r the Caribbean part of the Kingdom. It funds books and other publications
  that give substantial\, high-quality attention to an artist's work and pr
 actice. WHO CAN APPLY: visual artists and publishers\, individually or joi
 ntly\; the featured artist(s) must be professional and have been active fo
 r at least one year\, based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part of th
 e Kingdom. AMOUNT: the Mondriaan Fund contributes a maximum of 35% of the 
 eligible expenses\; the available budget for this round is EUR 205\,000. A
 PPLYING: submitted through the Mondriaan Fund's online application portal\
 , which requires a pre-created account (allow several working days to set 
 up). DEADLINE: 30 July 2026\, 16:00 Dutch time (10:00 Caribbean time).\n\n
 Organisation: Mondriaan Fund (Mondriaan Fonds)\nLocation: Netherlands. Fea
 tured artist(s) must be professional visual artists based in the Netherlan
 ds or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom\; publishers may co-apply.\nAward:
  Contribution of up to 35% of eligible expenses. Total budget for this rou
 nd is EUR 205\,000.\nDuration: Project-based (production of a publication)
 .\n\nMore: https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/publi
 cation-2/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/publication-2
 /?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands. Featured artist(s) must be professional visual artist
 s based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom\; publishe
 rs may co-apply.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-residency-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260827
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260828
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: Residency
DESCRIPTION:The Mondriaan Fund's Residency grant supports professional visu
 al artists\, curators\, and art observers who want to deepen their practic
 e through a residency in the Netherlands or abroad. It contributes to the 
 costs of a self-arranged residency that offers meaningful development for 
 the applicant's work. WHO CAN APPLY: visual artists\, curators\, and art o
 bservers who have been professionally active for at least one year and are
  based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom. AMOUNT: th
 e grant amount varies depending on the residency's location and duration\;
  applicants must contribute at least 10% of the costs themselves\, through
  personal investment\, third-party contributions\, an interest-free loan\,
  or crowdfunding. APPLYING: submitted through the Mondriaan Fund's online 
 application portal\, which requires a pre-created account (allow several w
 orking days). DEADLINE: 27 August 2026\, 16:00 Dutch time (11:00 Caribbean
  time).\n\nOrganisation: Mondriaan Fund (Mondriaan Fonds)\nLocation: Nethe
 rlands. Applicants must be based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part 
 of the Kingdom\; residencies may take place in the Netherlands or abroad.\
 nAward: Grant amount varies depending on the location and duration of the 
 residency. Applicants must contribute at least 10% themselves (personal in
 vestment\, third-party contributions\, an interest-free loan\, or crowdfun
 ding).\nDuration: Varies by residency.\n\nMore: https://www.mondriaanfonds
 .nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/residency/?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands. Applicants must be based in the Netherlands or the Ca
 ribbean part of the Kingdom\; residencies may take place in the Netherland
 s or abroad.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:rijkscultuurfondsen-interdisciplinary-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Rijkscultuurfondsen: Interdisciplinary Grant
DESCRIPTION:The Interdisciplinary grant is a new joint scheme of the six Du
 tch national cultural funds (Mondriaan Fund\, Performing Arts Fund NL\, Fu
 nd for Cultural Participation\, Netherlands Film Fund\, Creative Industrie
 s Fund NL\, and the Dutch Foundation for Literature)\, announced on 7 July
  2026. It supports makers and organisations whose work genuinely crosses t
 he boundaries of the individual funds' disciplines (visual art\, design\, 
 film\, literature\, performing arts\, and cultural participation) and cann
 ot be contained within a single discipline. TWO TRACKS: an Individual trac
 k for professional makers with a demonstrable interdisciplinary practice (
 at least one year of experience\; income cap EUR 55\,000 including the gra
 nt\; approximately EUR 24\,900 for 12 months)\; and an Organisation track 
 for organisations running multi-discipline projects. AMOUNT: EUR 12\,500 t
 o 50\,000 per project. ELIGIBILITY: makers and organisations based in the 
 Netherlands or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom. APPLYING: handled throug
 h the Mondriaan Fund's online application portal (info@mondriaanfonds.nl)\
 ; an account is required in advance. DEADLINE: 1 October 2026\, 16:00 Dutc
 h time (single application round).\n\nOrganisation: Dutch national cultura
 l funds (Rijkscultuurfondsen)\, via the Mondriaan Fund\nLocation: Netherla
 nds. For professional makers and organisations based in the Netherlands or
  the Caribbean part of the Kingdom whose practice spans multiple disciplin
 es.\nAward: EUR 12\,500 to 50\,000 per project. The individual-maker track
  is approximately EUR 24\,900 for 12 months (income cap EUR 55\,000 includ
 ing the grant).\nDuration: Up to 12 months for the individual-maker track.
 \n\nMore: https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/interd
 isciplinary/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/interdiscipli
 nary/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands. For professional makers and organisations based in th
 e Netherlands or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom whose practice spans mu
 ltiple disciplines.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:national-gallery-iceland-cusp-residency-reykjavik-2027@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260903
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260904
SUMMARY:Deadline: National Gallery of Iceland: CUSP Artist Residency (Reykj
 avik 2027)
DESCRIPTION:The National Gallery of Iceland announces an artist residency o
 pen call under its CUSP program\, a European cooperation project funded th
 rough Creative Europe's Culture grant. Artists working in complex media or
  emerging technologies (e.g. generative AI\, custom sensors\, sound\, soft
 ware-based\, blockchain\, physical and digital hybrid) are invited to appl
 y. This is the first of four residency stays offered to different artists 
 through the program\, one in each of the participating countries (Iceland\
 , Norway\, Czechia\, and Ukraine)\, followed by two micro residencies in t
 he Netherlands and Iceland. The programme brings together artists working 
 in time-based media with museum professionals\, conservators\, and technol
 ogy specialists developing preservation and conservation methodologies to 
 ensure the long-term endurance of artworks. The selected artist will meet 
 with a preservation specialist or conservator specializing in time-based m
 edia art for an in-depth artist interview addressing the preservation need
 s of their medium\, and will present their body of work and working method
 s publicly during an open-lab masterclass designed in collaboration with t
 he museum. RESIDENCY: up to four weeks in early 2027 in Reykjavik\, exact 
 dates arranged with the selected artist. WHAT IS PROVIDED: accommodation a
 nd working space\; a travel and subsistence stipend\; an artist fee for th
 e open lab/masterclass\; and possibly hardware and software depending on t
 he artist's request and museum means. ELIGIBILITY: international artists b
 ased outside Iceland\; applicants must be residents of any of the 27 EU me
 mber states or of non-EU countries with association agreements (EFTA/EEA m
 embers\, acceding countries\, candidate countries\, and European Neighbour
 hood Policy countries). APPLICATION: complete the application form with fu
 ll name\, country of residence\, gender identity\, contact information\, a
 n artistic statement (max 2500 characters)\, a motivation letter (max 2500
  characters)\, a draft idea of the public workshop or masterclass (max 100
 0 characters)\, links to social media/professional websites\, and a CV and
  portfolio upload (max 10MB each). DEADLINE: 3 September 2026 at 23:59 GMT
 . ABOUT CUSP: Creative Understanding\, Saving and Preserving Time-Based Me
 dia Art is a transnational cooperation project co-funded by the EU's Creat
 ive Europe Culture grant\, led by the National Gallery of Iceland with Nas
 jonalmuseet (Norway)\, LI-MA (Netherlands)\, Vasulka Kitchen (Czechia)\, a
 nd Mystetskyi Art Arsenal (Ukraine)\, focused on preserving and presenting
  time-based media art. Inquiries: cusp.residency.is [at] gmail.com.\n\nOrg
 anisation: National Gallery of Iceland (Listasafn Islands)\, CUSP project\
 nLocation: Reykjavik\, Iceland. Residency hosted by the National Gallery o
 f Iceland.\nAward: Accommodation and working space for the residency\; tra
 vel and subsistence stipend\; an artist fee for collaborating in the open-
 lab/masterclass\; and\, depending on the artist's request and museum means
 \, hardware and software for the working space. No application fee.\nDurat
 ion: Up to four weeks\, taking place in early 2027\, with exact dates arra
 nged with the selected artist.\n\nMore: https://www.listasafn.is/en/art/ne
 ws-vasulka-stofa/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
URL:https://www.listasafn.is/en/art/news-vasulka-stofa/?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Reykjavik\, Iceland. Residency hosted by the National Gallery of I
 celand.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:royal-conservatoire-antwerp-research-proposals-2027@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260921
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260922
SUMMARY:Deadline: Royal Conservatoire Antwerp: Call for Artistic Research P
 roposals (2027 start)
DESCRIPTION:The Royal Conservatoire Antwerp (AP University of the Arts) inv
 ites applications for artistic research projects starting 20 September 202
 7\, in three types: start projects\, one-year projects\, and two-year rese
 arch projects. Applications are embedded in one or more of the Conservatoi
 re's four research groups\, supervised by a supervisor\, and must align wi
 th the Conservatoire's mission and vision. FOCAL POINTS across disciplines
 : research into improvisation and intuitive creation and performance pract
 ices\; new dramaturgical and scenographic approaches in cross-over project
 s\; methodologies for interdisciplinary creation processes\; sustainable r
 elationships between artists and urban communities\; the possibilities and
  limitations of inclusion in education and/or on stage\; and well-being an
 d resilience in music and performing arts. FUNDING (salaried research posi
 tion): start projects\, 20% employment for 12 months plus EUR 1\,000 opera
 ting resources\; one-year projects\, 55% employment for 12 months plus EUR
  1\,000\; two-year projects\, 55% employment for 24 months plus EUR 2\,000
 . PRECONDITIONS: the applicant holds a relevant master's degree and conduc
 ts research situated in or arising from their own artistic practice with t
 he capacity for discursivity\; the proposal must align with the Conservato
 ire's mission/vision\; the applicant must be able to enter contractual emp
 loyment with the Royal Conservatoire Antwerp/AP University of the Arts for
  the duration of the project (international applicants must hold a valid B
 elgian work permit)\; proposals may be submitted only in Dutch or English.
  PROCEDURE (two steps): STEP 1\, submit an expression of interest by 21 Se
 ptember 2026\, 12:00 noon (applicants informed by the week of 5 October wh
 ether they may develop a full proposal with a research group)\; STEP 2\, s
 ubmit the full project dossier by 1 December 2026\, 12:00 noon. Evaluation
  by an independent reading committee aims to conclude in March 2027. Conta
 ct: Kevin Voets\, Head of Research\, kevin.voets@ap.be.\n\nOrganisation: R
 oyal Conservatoire Antwerp (AP University of the Arts)\nLocation: Antwerp\
 , Belgium. Salaried research position\; international applicants must hold
  a valid Belgian work permit.\nAward: Salaried research position (contract
