BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:art-explora-cite-internationale-des-arts-2027@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261106
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261107
SUMMARY:Deadline: Art Explora x Cite internationale des arts Residency Prog
 ramme\, 2027 Sessions
DESCRIPTION:Residency programme run by the Art Explora Foundation with the 
 Cite internationale des arts at its Montmartre site\, supporting work that
  engages scientific and technological exploration and the major social and
  environmental issues of the present. Residents receive a studio-apartment
  of around 50 sqm\, a monthly living grant of EUR 1\,000\, a production gr
 ant of up to EUR 3\,000\, and a round-trip travel grant\, plus access to p
 roduction facilities and local networks. Two tracks are offered: a SOLO pr
 ogramme for artists and writers across visual arts\, performance\, digital
  practices\, literary and other experimental disciplines\, and a COLLECTIV
 E programme for duos or collectives working collaboratively on interdiscip
 linary\, experimental or research-based projects\, for which a single stud
 io-apartment designed for collaborative work hosts two residents sharing a
  bed\, and only one member should submit the application. Eligible artisti
 c disciplines are visual arts\, performance and live arts\, digital arts\,
  curatorial practices\, and writing and literary practices\; eligible rese
 arch fields are humanities\, social sciences\, cultural studies and histor
 y\, technological innovation\, and ecology. No output is required at the e
 nd of the residency\, but participation in the Open Studios is mandatory. 
 ELIGIBILITY: artists\, artist collectives\, researchers\, curators and wri
 ters of all nationalities\, no age restriction\, with a minimum of five ye
 ars of professional activity and works or research exhibited at national o
 r international level. The Cite lists 20 to 22 residencies per year on thi
 s programme. Deadline 6 November 2026\, 23:59 Europe/Paris. Questions: res
 idences@artexplora.org. FLAG: the grant figures above come from the open-c
 all text supplied to the desk\; neither the Cite nor Art Explora publishes
  amounts on the programme page\, so confirm them\, along with the exact de
 adline\, before applying.\n\nOrganisation: Art Explora Foundation with the
  Cite internationale des arts\nLocation: Paris\, France (Cite internationa
 le des arts\, Montmartre site\, 24 rue Norvins\, 75018). Open to applicant
 s of all nationalities with no age restriction.\nAward: Monthly living gra
 nt of EUR 1\,000\, production grant of up to EUR 3\,000\, a round-trip tra
 vel grant\, and a fully equipped studio-apartment of roughly 50 sqm. For c
 ollectives and duos the living grant (up to EUR 2\,000) and the production
  grant (up to EUR 3\,000) are shared between members. Access to production
  facilities and local networks. No application fee.\nDuration: 3 to 6 mont
 hs. Session 1: March to August 2027. Session 2: September 2027 to February
  2028.\n\nMore: https://www.citeinternationaledesarts.fr/en/programme-de-r
 esidence/art-explora-x-cite-internationale-des-arts/?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.citeinternationaledesarts.fr/en/programme-de-residence/art-
 explora-x-cite-internationale-des-arts/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Paris\, France (Cite internationale des arts\, Montmartre site\, 2
 4 rue Norvins\, 75018). Open to applicants of all nationalities with no ag
 e restriction.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:vbkoe-refusing-rehearsing-resisting-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: Refusing Rehearsing Resisting: VBKÖ Annual Programme Ope
 n Call 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:VBKÖ\, the Austrian Association of Wom*n Artists founded in 19
 10\, invites proposals for its annual programme running March 2027 to Febr
 uary 2028. The call reads the present moment through the resurgence of aut
 horitarianism\, fascism\, militarisation\, colonial violence and extractiv
 e capitalism\, and the parallel privatisation of care and criminalisation 
 of solidarity. Drawing on María Galindo's framing of feminism as a projec
 t of social transformation and Sara Ahmed's account of feminist work begin
 ning with refusal\, the programme treats alternative ways of living as som
 ething rehearsed collectively through organising\, creating\, caring\, lis
 tening\, remembering and struggling. VBKÖ seeks a programme of practices 
 that nurture communal care and build a platform for collective rehearsals:
  proposals that allow for tenderness\, that are not afraid of ugly feeling
 s and different truths\, and that explore how contemporary queer and femin
 ist perspectives resonate across artistic\, social and political movements
 . FORMAT REQUIREMENT: only exhibition formats bringing together multiple a
 rtists\, collectives or collaborative practices are accepted. Solo exhibit
 ions\, individual presentations and projects centred on a single artist's 
 work are explicitly not supported. Proposals that activate the exhibition 
 through public programming (screenings\, performances\, workshops\, lectur
 es and other events) are encouraged. Regular opening hours are Fridays and
  Saturdays 2pm to 6pm\; any additional hours must be arranged and staffed 
 by the selected group. The association acknowledges that its own history i
 s marked by violence\, exclusion and discrimination on the basis of religi
 on\, class\, gender\, sexuality and ethnicity\, and states that it activel
 y opposes all forms of discrimination including trans-exclusionary radical
  feminism. APPLICATION: a single PDF (max 5MB) with a programme descriptio
 n (max 2 pages\, images welcome\, English or German\, including preferred 
 dates and duration)\, a CV and biography for every participant (max 1 page
  each)\, a financial plan (Fair Pay encouraged)\, technical requirements\,
  and contact details including email\, mobile number and main residence\; 
 three high-resolution images of work or exhibition sketches sent within th
 e email or via a download link\, labelled with artist name\, work title\, 
 year and proposal title (video links may be included in the proposal). Sen
 d to opencall@vbkoe.org with the subject line 'Open Call + your proposal t
 itle'. Incomplete applications will not be considered. A shortlist is comp
 iled by the VBKÖ team and final selections are made by the VBKÖ board wi
 th an external jury member experienced in contemporary art\, activism and 
 community building\; shortlisted applicants may be asked to present to the
  jury. Results are announced at the beginning of October 2026. Deadline 10
  September 2026\, 23:59. Free to apply.\n\nOrganisation: VBKÖ (Vereinigun
 g bildender Künstler*innen Österreichs\, Austrian Association of Wom*n A
 rtists)\nLocation: Vienna\, Austria (VBKÖ exhibition spaces and shared ki
 tchen)\nAward: EUR 4\,000 total budget per selected project\, covering bot
 h artist fees and production costs (fees\, materials\, labour\, production
 \, transport and other project expenses). Participants based outside Vienn
 a must include travel\, accommodation and shipping within that budget or s
 ecure external funding\; VBKÖ provides invitation and support letters to 
 help raise additional money. Also provided: the spaces and shared kitchen\
 , graphic design of online promotional material\, press announcements\, ph
 oto documentation\, up to 10 hours of technical setup support\, and techni
 cal equipment. Installation and de-installation are the selected group's r
 esponsibility. No application fee.\nDuration: 4 to 6 weeks\, taking place 
 between March 2027 and February 2028\n\nMore: https://www.vbkoe.org/en/ope
 n-call-refusing-rehearsing-resisting/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.vbkoe.org/en/open-call-refusing-rehearsing-resisting/?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Vienna\, Austria (VBKÖ exhibition spaces and shared kitchen)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:waag-trust-me-im-an-airtist-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Trust me\, I'm an AI/rtist: Open Call for Creative Interv
 entions
DESCRIPTION:Waag Futurelab invites artists\, theatre makers\, choreographer
 s\, dancers\, game designers and other creative practitioners to engage di
 rectly with seven experimental generative AI Enablers being co-created wit
 h technologists and dance\, theatre and gaming professionals. The call is 
 an invitation into the development process: selected artists get access to
  the technologies\, datasets\, interfaces and assumptions that shape these
  systems while they are still being built. Rather than testing whether the
  systems work\, artists are asked to interrogate why they work the way the
 y do\, who they are designed for\, what forms of creativity they enable or
  constrain\, and what assumptions about culture\, intelligence\, authorshi
 p\, labour\, bodies\, audiences and society are embedded in them. Waag is 
 looking for individuals and collectives willing to move beyond adoption in
 to investigation\, and welcomes proposals that are speculative\, critical\
 , poetic\, provocative\, humorous\, performative\, constructive or disrupt
 ive\, including work that challenges\, misuses\, reimagines\, hacks\, subv
 erts or plays with the tools\, exposes hidden biases\, questions environme
 ntal costs\, or imagines alternative technological futures. THE SEVEN AI E
 NABLERS\, each requiring an on-site visit to its technology partner: the G
 eneric Procedural Content Generation Enabler (text-to-3D assets and avatar
 s\, CERTH\, Greece)\; the Data-Driven Cultural Content Adaptation Enabler 
 (audience-aware creative content ideation with ranked candidate ideas\, CE
 RTH\, Greece)\; the Cross-Domain Audience Engagement Analytics Enabler (tu
 rning heterogeneous cultural participation data into interpretable engagem
 ent signals\, The Audience Agency\, Warrington\, UK)\; the Dance Style Ana
 lysis and Generation Enabler (dance video into virtual 3D choreography\, d
 issected into body\, effort\, shape and space components\, Centre of Excel
 lence for Research & Innovation\, Cyprus)\; the Immersive Design Integrati
 on Enabler for Dance & Theatre (NLP-driven XR environments with speech-to-
 3D scene editing\, Cyprus)\; the Music-to-Movement Translation Enabler (au
 dio into a time-aligned feature set spanning genre classification\, emotio
 nal valence-arousal analysis\, rhythm extraction and latent-space projecti
 on\, feeding motion generation\, spatial audio and visual choreography\, A
 ristotle University of Thessaloniki\, Greece)\; and the Adaptive Narrative
  Enabler for Games & Theatre (an evolutionary AI trained on creator-writte
 n story elements that generates infinite story variations\, explicitly rej
 ecting LLMs because of their dependence on copyrighted or public-domain co
 rpora that encode 19th and early 20th century sexism\, racism and imperial
 ism\, CAUSA Creation\, Austria). ELIGIBILITY: artists\, designers\, creati
 ve technologists\, theatre makers\, dancers\, choreographers and game desi
 gners\, as individuals or collectives\; applicants must hold a European VA
 T number. An info session with live demonstrations of the AI Enablers runs
  31 July 2026\, 10:00 to 11:30 CEST (meeting ID 995 7016 2341). Applicatio
 ns go through Homerun using the official Application Template\, uploaded a
 s a PDF\; read the Guide for Applicants\, which contains the full Enabler 
 descriptions\, before applying. Questions: zoenie at waag dot org. The AI 
 Enablers and this call are part of the Horizon Europe project HAMLET\, whi
 ch aims to make generative AI accessible to cultural organisations of all 
 sizes. Closes 30 September 2026\, 23:59 CEST. Free to apply.\n\nOrganisati
 on: Waag Futurelab (Horizon Europe project HAMLET)\nLocation: Europe. Enga
 ging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site visit to its technolog
 y partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warrington\, UK)\, the Ce
 ntre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\, Aristotle Universi
 ty of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austria). Final presentat
 ions September 2027.\nAward: EUR 20\,000 commission fee excluding VAT\, in
 clusive of artist fee\, travel\, accommodation and production costs. Appli
 cants must have a VAT number in Europe. No application fee.\nDuration: Jan
 uary to September 2027\, with final presentations in September 2027\n\nMor
 e: https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Europe. Engaging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site vi
 sit to its technology partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warri
 ngton\, UK)\, the Centre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\
 , Aristotle University of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austr
 ia). Final presentations September 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:uva-phd-sound-of-disagreement-nordics-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260820
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260821
SUMMARY:Deadline: University of Amsterdam: PhD\, Protest and Political Spee
 ch in the Nordics 1930-1970 (Sound of Disagreement)
DESCRIPTION:Fully funded four-year PhD position within 'The Sound of Disagr
 eement: Embodied Histories of Political Speech 1800-present'\, an interdis
 ciplinary project on how political groups have argued in public and how di
 sagreement sounds. This sub-project covers Nordic labour movements and the
  articulation of protest from roughly 1930 to 1970\, explicitly working on
  the sonic practices of political debate and the mediatization of protest\
 , so it suits researchers with a sound studies\, media studies or oral his
 tory angle as much as historians. REQUIREMENTS: a master's degree in Histo
 ry\, European Studies\, Media Studies\, Nordic Studies or a related discip
 line\; fluency in Danish\, Finnish\, Norwegian or Swedish depending on the
  chosen case study\; professional English\; archival research skills\; and
  a collaborative disposition. Apply via the AcademicTransfer posting (vaca
 ncy number 15290) with a motivation letter\, CV and research statement. De
 adline 20 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: University of Amsterdam (UvA)\, Fa
 culty of Humanities\, Department of European Studies\nLocation: Amsterdam\
 , Netherlands (Kloveniersburgwal 48)\nAward: EUR 3\,059 to 3\,881 gross pe
 r month at 38 hours per week\, on the Dutch university PhD scale\, plus 8 
 percent holiday allowance and 8.3 percent year-end bonus. No application f
 ee.\nDuration: 48 months full-time (16-month initial contract\, extended b
 y 32 months after a positive evaluation)\; preferred start 1 January 2027\
 n\nMore: https://www.academictransfer.com/en/jobs/362349/phd-position-on-p
 rotest-and-political-speech-in-the-nordics-1930-1970/?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.academictransfer.com/en/jobs/362349/phd-position-on-protest
 -and-political-speech-in-the-nordics-1930-1970/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Amsterdam\, Netherlands (Kloveniersburgwal 48)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:uu-phd-media-democracy-1970s-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Utrecht University: PhD\, Media and Democracy in the 1970
 s (NWO Vidi project)
DESCRIPTION:Fully funded PhD position in the NWO-funded Vidi project 'Democ
 racy and the (de-)regulation of the media in Europe since the 1970s'\, whi
 ch reads the contestation and transformation of democracy in the last quar
 ter of the twentieth century through media governance. This sub-project ex
 amines how social movements\, pirate broadcasters and alternative media in
 itiatives challenged the public broadcasting monopoly across Western Europ
 e during the long 1970s\, drawing on archival research. REQUIREMENTS: a ma
 ster's degree in history or a related field\, a demonstrated interest in t
 he history of democracy and media governance\, professional English plus a
 t least one other European language\, archival research experience and a c
 ollaborative approach. Apply through the AcademicTransfer posting (vacancy
  number 5535). Project leader Dr Pepijn Corduwener (p.corduwener@uu.nl). D
 eadline 6 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Utrecht University\, Faculty of
  Humanities\, Department of History and Art History\nLocation: Utrecht\, N
 etherlands (Drift 6)\nAward: EUR 3\,059 to 3\,881 gross per month at 36 to
  40 hours per week\, on the Dutch university PhD scale\, plus 8 percent ho
 liday allowance and 8.3 percent year-end bonus. No application fee.\nDurat
 ion: 18-month initial contract\, extended to four years after a positive f
 irst-year assessment\n\nMore: https://www.academictransfer.com/en/jobs/362
 200/phd-media-and-democracy-in-the-1970s/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.academictransfer.com/en/jobs/362200/phd-media-and-democracy
 -in-the-1970s/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Utrecht\, Netherlands (Drift 6)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:martin-roth-initiative-relocation-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260911
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260912
SUMMARY:Deadline: Martin Roth-Initiative: Temporary Relocation for Artists 
 and Cultural Actors at Risk (Programme Line 1\, 2026 call)
DESCRIPTION:The Martin Roth-Initiative is the German temporary-relocation p
 rogramme for artists and cultural actors at risk\, run jointly by the Goet
 he-Institut and ifa with Federal Foreign Office funding. Programme Line 1 
 relocates an artist to Germany for up to 15 months on a scholarship\, with
  insurance\, language courses\, psychosocial support\, counselling and vis
 a assistance\, plus personnel funding for the hosting organisation. ELIGIB
 ILITY: artists and cultural practitioners anywhere in the world who are de
 monstrably committed through their work to an open society\, freedom\, pea
 ce and democracy\; who face threats\, persecution or unsafe living conditi
 ons\; who have no access to a safe place to continue working\; and who spe
 ak English or German. Applicants must still be active in their home countr
 y or have left it after 20 February 2026. Citizens of EU and EEA countries
 \, Australia\, Canada\, Israel\, New Zealand\, Switzerland\, the UK and th
 e USA are excluded\, as are residents of Gaza and previous MRI recipients.
  HOST REQUIREMENT: applications are submitted jointly with a non-profit ho
 st organisation in Germany (museum\, theatre\, festival\, cultural collect
 ive\, NGO) that is a legal entity able to handle the administrative and di
 gital-security requirements. Note that the scholarship visa does not itsel
 f permit employment or self-employment in Germany. TIMELINE: applications 
 6 July to 11 September 2026\; decisions end of November 2026\; funding sta
 rts no earlier than 15 January 2027. Apply to apply@mri-application.de (en
 crypted email recommended)\; phone +49 30 284491-77.\n\nOrganisation: Mart
 in Roth-Initiative (Goethe-Institut and ifa\, funded by the German Federal
  Foreign Office)\nLocation: Germany (relocation to a German host organisat
 ion). Open to artists and cultural actors worldwide\, with exclusions.\nAw
 ard: Monthly scholarship for the relocated artist\, plus insurance\, langu
 age courses\, psychosocial support\, counselling and training\, visa suppo
 rt\, and personnel funding for the host organisation. No application fee.\
 nDuration: Up to 15 months\, starting no earlier than 15 January 2027\n\nM
 ore: https://www.martin-roth-initiative.de/en/application-programme-line-1
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.martin-roth-initiative.de/en/application-programme-line-1?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Germany (relocation to a German host organisation). Open to artist
 s and cultural actors worldwide\, with exclusions.
END:VEVENT
