UID:k3-tanzplan-hamburg-choreography-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260812
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260813
SUMMARY:Deadline: K3 | Tanzplan Hamburg: Choreography Residency 2027-2028
DESCRIPTION:K3 in Hamburg is one of the better-funded choreography residenc
 ies in Europe\, and it is aimed squarely at people who want research time 
 rather than a production deadline. Three choreographers are selected for e
 ight months at Kampnagel with a monthly scholarship\, studio access\, a pr
 oduction budget\, and dramaturgical\, technical and PR support\, plus outr
 each work and a public presentation at the end. Selection runs in two stag
 es. ELIGIBILITY: individual choreographers at an emerging to early mid-car
 eer stage who have already produced independent choreographic work outside
  an educational context. Open internationally\; formal dance training is n
 ot required. Teams and ensembles are not eligible\, only individuals. Appl
 ications close 12 August 2026 at 16:00 CEST.\n\nOrganisation: K3 | Tanzpla
 n Hamburg (Kampnagel)\nLocation: Hamburg\, Germany. Open internationally.\
 nAward: Monthly scholarship (EUR 2\,500 per month in the 2025/26 cycle\; t
 he 2027/28 figure had not been announced at the time of listing)\, plus on
 e-time travel expenses\, visa assistance\, a moderate production budget\, 
 studio access\, and dramaturgical\, technical and PR support. No applicati
 on fee is stated.\nDuration: Eight months\, August 2027 to April 2028\n\nM
 ore: https://www.k3-hamburg.de/en/k3/jobs&callforproposals?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.k3-hamburg.de/en/k3/jobs&callforproposals?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Hamburg\, Germany. Open internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-fears-berlin-performance-art-award-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: NEW FEARS Gallery: Berlin Performance Art Award 2026
DESCRIPTION:NEW FEARS is a Berlin gallery set up as a hybrid space for arti
 sts working across choreography and visual art\, with a focus on body-base
 d practice\, and this award extends that to artists working outside conven
 tional theatre and institutional structures. A jury of Bryana Fritz\, Jaco
 po Lanteri and Burcu Bilgic shortlists six projects\, each of which perfor
 ms live at the gallery on 16 or 17 October 2026\; three winners are then c
 hosen and celebrated at Panke Club on 18 October. The application is unusu
 ally light: name\, email\, an optional text of up to 500 words about your 
 work\, and up to six links to video documentation with passwords where nee
 ded. No CV\, no written proposal\, no budget\, which means the video docum
 entation carries the whole application\, so lead with work that shows imme
 diately how it operates live in a gallery. ELIGIBILITY: Berlin-based artis
 ts\, duos and groups working across performance\, choreography\, visual ar
 t or body-based practice. You must be in Berlin for setup the week before 
 and for the presentations on 16-17 October 2026\, and be able to issue an 
 invoice for the award payment. Existing or new work is fine\, with no stat
 ed duration limits. AVAILABLE EQUIPMENT: sound system\, speakers\, mixer\,
  LED lights\, projector\, wind machine\, smoke machine\, microphone and ba
 sic cables\; check the venue PDF before proposing anything that depends on
  a particular room\, access route or technical setup. NOTE: the organisers
  describe this as a DIY\, near-zero-budget initiative. There is no product
 ion budget and nothing beyond the grant\, so a group splitting EUR 1\,300 
 should go in with eyes open. TIMELINE: applications close 15 August 2026 (
 no time or timezone given)\; shortlist announced 15 September 2026. Apply 
 through the JotForm at form.jotform.com/261674665583066.\n\nOrganisation: 
 NEW FEARS Gallery\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany (NEW FEARS Gallery\, Reichen
 bergerstrasse 114\, 10999 Berlin). Berlin-based applicants only.\nAward: T
 hree winning projects each receive a EUR 1\,300 grant\, shared between the
  members of a duo or group\, plus a ten-day residency at NEW FEARS Gallery
 \, basic technical support and access to the gallery's equipment. The thre
 e shortlisted projects that do not win divide 50 percent of the entrance r
 evenue equally. Travel\, accommodation\, materials\, shipping\, insurance\
 , per diem and production costs are NOT covered\, and artists with a non-G
 erman tax number may see tax deducted from the prize at an unstated rate. 
 No application fee.\nDuration: Ten-day residency scheduled with the winner
 s during 2026 or 2027\; setup the week before 16 October\, public performa
 nces 16-17 October 2026\n\nMore: https://www.newfears.net/?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.newfears.net/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany (NEW FEARS Gallery\, Reichenbergerstrasse 114\, 1
 0999 Berlin). Berlin-based applicants only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:drift-choreographer-open-call-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: DRIFT: Choreographer Open Call 2026-27 (Dance and Researc
 h In The Future Time)
DESCRIPTION:DRIFT (Dance and Research In The Future Time) is a four-year Cr
 eative Europe cooperation between ten European organisations\, led by Thea
 tre de Liege and Maison de la danse. The distinguishing feature is that it
  funds artistic research rather than production: you are expected to arriv
 e with questions\, not a finished piece you already intend to make\, and r
 eviewers read every section of the application through that lens. STRUCTUR
 E: each of the ten partner organisations selects one choreographer from it
 s own country\, forming a cohort of ten. Over the programme you get an Onb
 oarding Lab\, two weeks of residency with your national partner\, three co
 llaborative Online Labs\, a Festival Lab in Tunis\, individual artistic an
 d curatorial mentoring\, and a work-in-progress showcase at the Biennale d
 e la danse in Lyon\, all backed by a EUR 20\,000 research budget that cove
 rs your fees\, travel\, accommodation and any collaborators. SECOND PHASE:
  after a further evaluation\, five of the ten artists may receive co-produ
 ction support and touring across the DRIFT network. That phase is separate
  from this application and is not guaranteed. ELIGIBILITY: professional ch
 oreographers in contemporary dance or performance\, at a mid-career stage\
 , meaning an established national practice but not yet fully established i
 nternationally. You must be based in Belgium\, France\, Germany (Berlin re
 gion only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Italy\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tunisia or Portug
 al\, and you apply through the partner organisation representing your coun
 try of residence\, not centrally. Good English and availability for all pr
 ogramme dates are required. The country list is strict\, so check it befor
 e you start. TIMELINE: applications opened 1 July 2026 and close 31 August
  2026\; selection through September\; results 9 October 2026\; programme r
 uns November 2026 to September 2027.\n\nOrganisation: DRIFT\, a four-year 
 Creative Europe cooperation of ten organisations led by Theatre de Liege a
 nd Maison de la danse\nLocation: Activities across Europe: kick-off in Ber
 lin\, Festival Lab in Tunis\, showcase in Lyon\, online labs\, plus a resi
 dency hosted by your own national DRIFT partner. Applicants must be based 
 in Belgium\, France\, Germany (Berlin region only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Ita
 ly\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tunisia or Portugal\, and apply through the partner
  organisation for their country of residence.\nAward: EUR 20\,000 research
  budget per choreographer\, covering artistic fees\, travel\, accommodatio
 n and optional collaborator costs. Also included: an Onboarding Lab\, two 
 weeks of residency with your national DRIFT partner\, three collaborative 
 Online Labs\, a Festival Lab in Tunis\, individual artistic mentoring and 
 individual curatorial mentoring\, and a work-in-progress presentation at t
 he Biennale de la danse in Lyon. No application fee.\nDuration: November 2
 026 to September 2027. Kick-off in Berlin 30 November to 1 December 2026\;
  Festival Lab in Tunis 18-21 March 2027\; Biennale de la danse showcase in
  Lyon 18-25 September 2027.\n\nMore: https://driftdance.eu/artists/open-ca
 ll?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://driftdance.eu/artists/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Activities across Europe: kick-off in Berlin\, Festival Lab in Tun
 is\, showcase in Lyon\, online labs\, plus a residency hosted by your own 
 national DRIFT partner. Applicants must be based in Belgium\, France\, Ger
 many (Berlin region only)\, Greece\, Norway\, Italy\, Spain\, Ukraine\, Tu
 nisia or Portugal\, and apply through the partner organisation for their c
 ountry of residence.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:scuola-piccola-zattere-international-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260905
SUMMARY:Deadline: Scuola Piccola Zattere: International Fellowship and Resi
 dency 2027 (Venice)
DESCRIPTION:Scuola Piccola Zattere is an independent non-profit centre for 
 continuous education and research in the expanded field of contemporary ar
 ts\, running exhibitions\, commissions\, fellowships\, workshops\, seminar
 s\, performances and public discussions as one connected research ecosyste
 m rather than separate strands. Two international fellowships are offered.
  The support is unusually solid for a research fellowship: EUR 5\,000 as a
  grant\, EUR 2\,000 as a research budget\, accommodation\, travel and a st
 udy space for three months. SEASON THEME: the 2026-2027 cycle is titled Li
 ving Document\, investigating archives\, regulatory documents\, collective
  rituals\, games\, performative instructions\, and the relationship betwee
 n bodies\, architecture and social organisation. Fellows join that shared 
 inquiry while continuing their own work\, and are expected to keep an acti
 ve presence in the institution and contribute to public events through wor
 kshops\, seminars\, presentations\, screenings or performances. The fellow
 ship overlaps with the run-up to and opening of the 2027 Architecture Bien
 nale\, which is a real advantage if your research benefits from that traff
 ic. ELIGIBILITY: artists\, curators\, researchers\, architects\, choreogra
 phers\, performers and writers of any nationality\, as individuals\, colle
 ctives or duos. Good English is required\, and non-EU applicants need a va
 lid passport for international travel. No age limit\, but the organisers s
 ay it is especially intended for practitioners in the post-academic stage 
 of their career\, typically under 40. You must be prepared to spend the fu
 ll period in Venice\, so this is not a remote or partial-attendance fellow
 ship. WHAT THEY WANT: an active research process that can develop through 
 discussion and experimentation\, tied to the Living Document theme\, not a
  finished stand-alone project proposal. The motivation letter\, portfolio 
 and public programming proposal are read as one integrated application. De
 adline 4 September 2026 at 10:00 CEST.\n\nOrganisation: Scuola Piccola Zat
 tere\nLocation: Venice\, Italy. Open to any nationality.\nAward: EUR 5\,00
 0 fellowship grant plus a EUR 2\,000 research budget\, accommodation in Ve
 nice\, an individual study space within a shared studio\, and round-trip t
 ravel to Venice. Collectives and duos share a single grant and one accommo
 dation. No application fee.\nDuration: Three months\, in one of two slots:
  early January to early April 2027\, or mid-April to mid-July 2027. Applic
 ants may name one slot or indicate availability for both.\n\nMore: https:/
 /scuolapiccolazattere.com/en/events/internationalfellowship-2-3?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://scuolapiccolazattere.com/en/events/internationalfellowship-2-3?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Venice\, Italy. Open to any nationality.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:human-rights-watch-ai-strategy-solutions-lead-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Human Rights Watch: AI Strategy and Solutions Lead (one-y
 ear fixed term)
DESCRIPTION:Human Rights Watch is hiring an AI Strategy and Solutions Lead 
 into its Operations Department to build the foundation for responsible AI 
 adoption across the organisation. The role deliberately sits between strat
 egy and execution: assessing organisational readiness\, mapping partnershi
 ps and governance\, and also prototyping and shipping working systems. THE
  WORK\, in four strands. (1) STRATEGY AND READINESS: with the CIO and the 
 Information Security and Technology (IST) leadership team\, build an AI st
 rategy covering data foundation requirements\, infrastructure gaps and org
 anisational readiness\; assess current state and shape a roadmap balancing
  quick wins against long-term capacity\; research what peer nonprofit\, hu
 manitarian and human rights organisations are doing and find collaboration
  openings. (2) FLAGSHIP SOLUTIONS: work with departments to find high-impa
 ct use cases\, translate business needs into technical approaches\, build 
 and deploy AI/ML solutions with measurable value (time saved\, efficiency\
 , better outcomes)\, using the Microsoft AI ecosystem (Copilot Studio\, Az
 ure AI\, Power Platform) among other tools\, and oversee consultants and v
 endors to extend delivery capacity. (3) ADOPTION AND CAPACITY: balance onb
 oarding staff onto AI tools against building custom solutions where they a
 mplify the organisation's strengths\; write training material pitched at d
 ifferent work areas and skill levels covering prompting and responsible us
 e\; support working groups and communities of practice\; produce playbooks
  and guides for non-technical staff. (4) GOVERNANCE: partner with IST lead
 ership on risk assessment frameworks\, align solutions with security\, inf
 rastructure and architecture requirements\, contribute to AI policy develo
 pment and its operationalisation\, set standards for evaluating proofs of 
 concept and MVPs\, and engage business units so outcomes are co-owned\; al
 l of it balanced against data quality\, security and resource constraints 
 and HRW's mission as a human rights organisation. WHO THEY WANT: a bachelo
 r's degree or equivalent work experience in computer science\, data scienc
 e\, information systems\, mathematics\, AI/ML implementation or a related 
 technical field\; a minimum of seven years' relevant experience including 
 two to three years on AI and two to three years implementing AI/ML in prod
 uction\; experience contributing to technology strategy and assessing orga
 nisational readiness for new technology\; strong Python and ML library/fra
 mework skills\; hands-on predictive modelling\, data science and statistic
 al analysis\; experience with low-code/no-code AI platforms such as Copilo
 t Studio or Power Platform\; a track record of scoping and delivering unde
 r resource constraints\; a grasp of both generative AI and traditional ML\
 ; and the ability to explain technical concepts plainly to mixed audiences
 \, engage stakeholders and lead technology adoption as an internal partner
  to business teams. PREFERRED: nonprofit\, humanitarian or human rights ex
 perience\; data privacy\, security and ethical AI knowledge\; familiarity 
 with responsible AI frameworks and governance models\; vendor and consulta
 nt management\; and a background in politically sensitive or high-risk env
 ironments. APPLY: through the HRW job portal with a cover letter and CV\, 
 preferably as PDFs\, by 30 August 2026. Only complete applications are rev
 iewed and only shortlisted candidates are contacted\; no calls or email en
 quiries. HRW states that it does not discriminate on the basis of disabili
 ty\, age\, gender identity and expression\, national origin\, race and eth
 nicity\, religious beliefs\, sexual orientation or criminal record.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Human Rights Watch (HRW)\, Operations Department\nLocation: Mus
 t be within commuting distance of an HRW office: Amsterdam\, Amman\, Berli
 n\, Brussels\, London\, Stockholm\, New York\, Washington DC\, Miami\, Sao
  Paulo\, Toronto or Chicago. Reports to the Chief Information Officer in B
 erlin. No relocation support and no work-authorisation sponsorship.\nAward
 : USD 102\,000 to 112\,000 plus employer-paid benefits if based in the US\
 ; ranges outside the US are set by local market conditions. No application
  fee.\nDuration: One year fixed-term\, full time. Application deadline 30 
 August 2026.\n\nMore: https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/humanrightswatch/jo
 bs/8350654002?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/humanrightswatch/jobs/8350654002?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Must be within commuting distance of an HRW office: Amsterdam\, Am
 man\, Berlin\, Brussels\, London\, Stockholm\, New York\, Washington DC\, 
 Miami\, Sao Paulo\, Toronto or Chicago. Reports to the Chief Information O
 fficer in Berlin. No relocation support and no work-authorisation sponsors
 hip.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:julies-bicycle-iter-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: ITER 2026: International Touring and Environmental Respon
 sibility Programme
DESCRIPTION:ITER is now in its fifth year and supports people in the perfor
 ming arts to build environmentally responsible\, inclusive and equitable w
 ays of touring and collaborating internationally. It is initiated and fina
 nced by Arts Council England\, Arts Council Norway\, Creative Scotland and
  the Danish Arts Foundation\, and designed and delivered by Julie's Bicycl
 e and in futurum. Creative Scotland joined as a partner for 2026. TWO TRAC
 KS\, up to 60 places in total: PATHFINDERS\, ten sessions introducing the 
 principles of sustainable touring for people new to the subject\; and CHAN
 GEMAKERS\, nine sessions for those further along\, on leadership and syste
 mic change. Seminars run weekly from 1 October to 10 December 2026\, Pathf
 inders on Tuesdays and Changemakers on Thursdays\, 10:00 to 12:00 UK time\
 , alongside networking and a new alumni network. THE MONEY COMES IN PHASE 
 TWO: after the seminars\, groups of three or more participants can apply f
 or up to GBP 30\,000 to implement collaborative projects that cut environm
 ental impacts and advance climate justice. Funding applications open 6 Nov
 ember 2026 and close 11 January 2027\; awarded projects begin in spring 20
 27. ELIGIBILITY: based in England\, Scotland\, Norway or Denmark\, working
  in dance\, music or theatre. That covers music and theatre venues receivi
 ng touring work\, festivals\, orchestras\, theatre companies\, solo artist
 s\, performers and musicians\, performing arts producers\, and multidiscip
 linary arts organisations. Applications are made through the relevant nati
 onal funder (Arts Council England open funds\, Creative Scotland targeted 
 funding\, Arts Council Norway\, Danish Arts Foundation). Questions go to e
 nvironmentaltouring@artscouncil.org.uk.\n\nOrganisation: Julie's Bicycle a
 nd in futurum\, funded by Arts Council England\, Arts Council Norway\, Cre
 ative Scotland and the Danish Arts Foundation\nLocation: England\, Scotlan
 d\, Norway and Denmark. Seminars run online.\nAward: The seminar programme
  is free to join. Groups of three or more participants can then apply for 
 up to GBP 30\,000 for collaborative projects\, with funding applications o
 pen 6 November 2026 to 11 January 2027 and projects starting spring 2027. 
 No application fee.\nDuration: Seminars run weekly from 1 October to 10 De
 cember 2026\, Tuesdays for Pathfinders and Thursdays for Changemakers\, 10
 :00 to 12:00 UK time. Deadline to join 1 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://
 juliesbicycle.org/news/iter-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://juliesbicycle.org/news/iter-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:England\, Scotland\, Norway and Denmark. Seminars run online.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:connect-and-create-circulation-performing-arts-music-2026@artificialnou
 veau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Connect and Create: Circulation of Performing Arts and Mu
 sic (Africa-Europe) - 2nd Call
DESCRIPTION:The Circulation strand of Connect and Create funds the touring 
 and dissemination of African artistic creations across Africa and Europe. 
 It is part of the Africa-Europe Partnerships for Culture programme (EUR 8M
 \, led by the Goethe-Institut and Expertise France in partnership with Ins
 titut francais\, supported by the European Union)\, and is a different str
 and from the Professional Mobilities call also on the desk. THIS CARD IS T
 HE SECOND CALL\, deadline 30 September 2026 (the first call closed 30 Nove
 mber 2025). AMOUNT: EUR 15\,000 to EUR 39\,500 per project. WHAT IS FUNDED
 : mobility costs for artistic teams\, including transport\, visas\, insura
 nce\, lodging\, per diems\, local transport\, and shipping of scenographic
  and technical elements. WHO CAN APPLY: artistic teams (performing arts co
 mpanies\, music ensembles\, collectives) based in sub-Saharan Africa\; dis
 semination structures (production houses\, artist managers\, theatres\, la
 bels\, venues) based in Africa or Europe\; and event organisers (festivals
 \, markets\, professional gatherings) in Africa or Europe. Applicants must
  be legally established in one of the 47 eligible sub-Saharan African coun
 tries\, Kenya among them\, or one of the 27 EU Member States. INDIVIDUALS 
 CANNOT APPLY. ELIGIBLE PROJECTS\, two formats: (1) TOURS\, requiring a min
 imum of 5 pre-booked dates across at least 3 different African countries\,
  or at least 3 pre-booked dates in one or more European countries\; (2) PR
 OGRAMMING WITHIN MAJOR EVENTS\, requiring a festival\, market or professio
 nal gathering in Africa or Europe to host at least 3 artistic teams from d
 ifferent African countries. TIMELINE: activities must take place between F
 ebruary and December 2027\; selection committee December 2026\, results an
 nounced January 2027. PRIORITY is given to projects that build on existing
  tandems already collaborating\, feature emerging artists\, use eco-respon
 sible practices\, and address inclusion. APPLY via the Institut francais e
 mundus portal. Questions: connectandcreate@expertisefrance.fr.\n\nOrganisa
 tion: Goethe-Institut and Expertise France\, in collaboration with Institu
 t francais (Africa-Europe Partnerships for Culture consortium)\nLocation: 
 Applicants based in one of the 47 eligible sub-Saharan African countries (
 Kenya included) or one of the 27 EU Member States. Touring in Africa and/o
 r Europe.\nAward: EUR 15\,000 to EUR 39\,500 per project\, covering travel
 \, visas\, insurance\, accommodation\, per diems\, local transport and shi
 pping of scenographic and technical elements. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: Projects carried out between February and December 2027\n\nMore: https:
 //ifprog.emundus.fr/en/campagne-info?view=programme&cid=353&utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://ifprog.emundus.fr/en/campagne-info?view=programme&cid=353&utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicants based in one of the 47 eligible sub-Saharan African cou
 ntries (Kenya included) or one of the 27 EU Member States. Touring in Afri
 ca and/or Europe.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:titanik-mcult-residing-non-linear-technologies-sonic-worldings-2027@art
 ificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Residing Non-linear Technologies and Sonic Worldings (M-C
 ult x Titanik\, Helsinki and Turku)
DESCRIPTION:A new pilot residency from Art and Media Culture Agency M-Cult 
 in Helsinki and Arte artists' association / Titanik gallery in Turku\, sit
 ting at the intersection of art\, media and sonic practice\, funded by the
  Nordic-Baltic Mobility Programme for Culture from Nordic Culture Point. T
 WO ARTISTS will be selected. THE PREMISE: the programme treats technology 
 as a situated practice and deliberately shifts attention away from the pro
 gress narratives promoted by large global technology companies\, toward lo
 cal\, indigenous and community-oriented perspectives. It focuses on making
  vernacular and non-linear technological and sonic environments visible\, 
 foregrounding vibrant local histories and traditions across the Nordic and
  Baltic region\, and asks what non-linear sonic and media practice might l
 ook like in the current social\, political and cultural landscape. WHAT EA
 CH ARTIST RECEIVES: EUR 3\,500 artist fee\; EUR 2\,000 production budget\;
  accommodation for two months divided between Helsinki and Turku\, one mon
 th in each\; travel costs for one return ticket from a Nordic-Baltic count
 ry\; access to Titanik's residency studio space and a working desk at M-Cu
 lt's office during each stay\; mentoring and curatorial support from both 
 organisations' staff\; and networking into the Finnish media art and conte
 mporary art scenes. In return the artist develops in-depth or focused rese
 arch and presents their practice and research at two public events\, one i
 n each city. WHO CAN APPLY: individual artists only\, explicitly NOT colle
 ctives this round\; artists from or based in Finland\, Sweden\, Norway\, D
 enmark\, Iceland\, Aland\, the Faroe Islands\, Greenland\, Estonia\, Latvi
 a or Lithuania\; working in sound\, media and/or digital arts\, or the exp
 anded field of vernacular technologies\, all defined broadly by the organi
 sers. Applicants must be available to travel to Finland for the full two-m
 onth period across February and March 2027. The organisers welcome applica
 nts at all career stages and from all backgrounds. One submission per arti
 st: if two are sent\, only the later one is reviewed. HOW TO APPLY: submit
  via the form linked from the open call page with a brief letter of motiva
 tion including a research plan for the residency\, how it connects with th
 e programme's themes and what you would focus on (max 1-2 pages)\, your CV
 \, and a link to your portfolio. Applications in English only. DEADLINE: 3
 0 August 2026 at 23:59 UTC+3. Online interviews with shortlisted artists t
 ake place before 19 September 2026\, and all applicants are notified by em
 ail by the end of September 2026. JURY: Jussi Koitela\, Director of M-Cult
 \, Helsinki\, and Farbod Fakharzadeh\, Director of Arte ry and Titanik\, T
 urku. ACCOMMODATION AND ACCESSIBILITY: in Helsinki\, the Vallila Residency
  hosted by M-Cult is a 30 sqm apartment in a small wooden house with one r
 oom\, kitchen and bathroom with shower\; it is NOT fully accessible\, sitt
 ing on the first floor up stairs with no elevator (details from info@m-cul
 t.org). In Turku\, the Titanik A.i.R. apartment is 27 sqm on the top floor
  of a building with an elevator\, but the space including the bathroom is 
 not fully accessible (details from info@titanik.fi). A partner\, friend or
  child may join\, but their expenses are not covered. THE PARTNERS: M-Cult
 \, founded in 2000\, develops and presents art and media culture with a fo
 cus on the social and political dimensions of media and technology\, worki
 ng through art commissions\, residencies and learning programmes. Titanik\
 , established in Turku in 1988 by the artist-run organization Arte Ry\, ha
 s since 2006 run Titanik A.i.R.\, the first artist residency in Finland wi
 th a focus on sound art and sonic practices. CONTACT: Jussi Koitela (jussi
 .koitela@m-cult.org) or Farbod Fakharzadeh (director@arte.fi).\n\nOrganisa
 tion: Art and Media Culture Agency M-Cult (Helsinki) and Arte artists' ass
 ociation / Titanik gallery (Turku)\nLocation: Finland\; one month in Helsi
 nki and one month in Turku. Open to artists from or based in Nordic and Ba
 ltic countries.\nAward: EUR 3\,500 artist fee plus EUR 2\,000 production b
 udget\, two months' accommodation split between Helsinki and Turku\, and o
 ne return travel ticket from a Nordic-Baltic country. No application fee s
 tated.\nDuration: Two months\, February to March 2027 (one month in each c
 ity)\n\nMore: https://www.titanik.fi/residency-open-call-residing-non-line
 ar-technologies-and-sonic-worldings/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.titanik.fi/residency-open-call-residing-non-linear-technolo
 gies-and-sonic-worldings/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
LOCATION:Finland\; one month in Helsinki and one month in Turku. Open to ar
 tists from or based in Nordic and Baltic countries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:spazioserra-open-call-2026-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Open Call 2026/2027 'Where is the tomorrow we saw yesterd
 ay?' (spazioSERRA)
DESCRIPTION:spazioSERRA turns ten in 2027\, and this open call is framed as
  a deliberate break from how independent art usually asks artists to arriv
 e: with deadlines met\, ideas already defined\, and a project ready to be 
 exhibited. The call asks 'where is the tomorrow we saw yesterday?' and sta
 tes plainly that it is not looking for completed projects or stabilised vi
 sions\, but for practices\, processes\, obsessions and still-open question
 s. Site-specific installations\, performances\, editorial productions\, vi
 deos\, urban interventions\, hybrid collaborations and forms that are stil
 l undefined are all welcome\; the point is not the format but the urgency 
 running through it\, and the willingness to enter a shared research proces
 s that can slow down\, change direction and stay incomplete long enough to
  transform. WHAT IT LEADS TO: the call identifies artists\, collectives an
 d research practices with whom spazioSERRA may develop collaborations duri
 ng the 2027 exhibition and project season\, which may take the form of sol
 o or group exhibitions\, site-specific installations\, performances\, edit
 orial productions\, video projects\, collaborations with institutions or e
 xternal organisations\, and further projects developed together. If select
 ed\, the artist or collective begins a curatorial dialogue with spazioSERR
 A aimed at developing an exhibition and/or additional projects\, jointly d
 efined during the collaboration. FUNDING: each selected project receives a
  financial contribution of EUR 500 to EUR 2\,000\, determined by the proje
 ct's characteristics and available resources\, covering production costs o
 f the work (which remains the property of the artist or collective)\, trav
 el\, food and accommodation\, and communication and promotion materials. T
 he contribution is paid at the end of the collaboration unless otherwise a
 greed\, and spazioSERRA reimburses only documented expenses paid by tracea
 ble methods with the relevant tax documentation. It is explicitly a contri
 bution towards the realisation of the project and/or the practice\, not ne
 cessarily full coverage of production costs\; if the project is developed 
 with external partners bringing additional resources\, the budget may be s
 upplemented by mutual agreement. Use of the exhibition space and the work 
 of the spazioSERRA team cost the artist nothing. SELECTION: no exhibition 
 project or defined proposal is required\; the jury assesses the artistic r
 esearch as a whole\, including the applicant's path\, the languages used\,
  the questions explored\, the potential for development and compatibility 
 with spazioSERRA's curatorial vision. The selecting curatorial collective 
 is Pietro Agostini\, Lidia Barca\, Tracy Bassil\, Virginia Dal Magro\, Mas
 similiano Fanto\, Camilla Gurgone\, Giulia Maffioli Brigatti\, Cristiano R
 izzo\, Victoria Tincati and Valentina Toccaceli. First selection in Novemb
 er 2026\, interviews November to December 2026\, final notification by Dec
 ember 2026. APPLICATION: online form open 15 July to 6 September 2026\, wi
 th a single PDF containing statement\, artistic CV and a selection of work
 s\, shared via a stable online link viewable without download\, authorisat
 ion or password\; collectives list all members. FEE: EUR 12 membership fee
  per person\, multiplied by the number of members for a collective. Contac
 t: info.spazioserra@gmail.com.\n\nOrganisation: spazioSERRA\nLocation: Mil
 an\, Italy. spazioSERRA operates inside the Milano Lancetti passante railw
 ay station\; registered office Via Maloia 1\, 20158 Milan. Open to artists
  and collectives of any nationality\, aged 18 or over.\nAward: EUR 500 to 
 EUR 2\,000 financial contribution per selected project\, set according to 
 the project's characteristics and available resources\, paid at the end of
  the collaboration against documented expenses with traceable payment meth
 ods. No cost to the artist for use of the exhibition space or for the work
  of the spazioSERRA team. Application fee: EUR 12 membership fee per perso
 n (multiplied by the number of members for collectives).\nDuration: Collab
 orations developed across the 2027 exhibition and project season\n\nMore: 
 https://www.spazioserra.org/en/exhibitions/open-call-2026-2027?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.spazioserra.org/en/exhibitions/open-call-2026-2027?utm_sour
 ce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Milan\, Italy. spazioSERRA operates inside the Milano Lancetti pas
 sante railway station\; registered office Via Maloia 1\, 20158 Milan. Open
  to artists and collectives of any nationality\, aged 18 or over.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:nordic-currents-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Nordic Currents 2027-2028 Open Call (Nordic Council of Mi
 nisters)
DESCRIPTION:Nordic Currents is a new international culture initiative from 
 the Nordic Council of Ministers\, running 2027-2028\, that invites cultura
 l organisations in European countries neighbouring the Nordic region to jo
 in forces with Nordic partners on projects that move audiences\, deepen re
 lationships and create lasting connections. Trust\, openness\, artistic fr
 eedom and democratic values are named as its core. The stated aims are to 
 showcase the richness and quality of contemporary Nordic culture and art i
 n neighbouring European countries and to build long-term collaborations be
 tween Nordic and European actors. WHO CAN APPLY: established organisations
  based in European countries neighbouring the Nordic region\, primarily Be
 lgium\, Estonia\, France\, Germany\, Ireland\, Lithuania\, Latvia\, the Ne
 therlands\, Poland and the United Kingdom. Eligible applicant types are cu
 ltural organisations\, institutions\, promoters\, festivals and producers\
 , as well as embassies and diplomatic missions working in cooperation with
  arts and culture stakeholders. Every application must have at least two p
 artners from different Nordic countries who stay active throughout the pro
 ject. WHERE: in a single country or as a cooperation across several countr
 ies\, but activities must primarily take place in the country where the ap
 plying organisation is based\; if several European partners outside the No
 rdic region are involved\, activities may also be organised in their count
 ries. Emphasis goes to projects in regions and countries with existing loc
 al interest in Nordic art and culture\, where Nordic and local partners ca
 n build long-term\, mutually beneficial collaborations. WHAT THE PROJECT M
 UST INCLUDE: both artistic programmes and events accessible to local audie
 nces\, and platforms for professional exchange and sector development. Pub
 lic cultural events can be festivals\, programmes\, series or other curate
 d formats\, standalone or attached to existing structures. Projects must a
 lso include network-building\, knowledge sharing and other forms of sector
  development. Programming must include representation from all the Nordic 
 countries\, with artists and cultural actors from Denmark\, the Faroe Isla
 nds\, Finland\, Greenland (Kalaallit Nunaat)\, Iceland\, Norway\, Sweden a
 nd Aland. Projects must take place between 1 June 2027 and 1 June 2028. FU
 NDING: applications may request EUR 100\,000 to EUR 800\,000\; the total p
 ool is EUR 800\,000\, so between one and eight proposals will be successfu
 l. Co-financing through local\, national or other funding sources is a pre
 requisite for eligibility. HOW TO APPLY: proposals are submitted in Englis
 h through an online portal open only between 1 October and 1 November 2026
 . ASSESSMENT: all eligible proposals go to a professional committee and ex
 pert reviewers in the corresponding art fields\, judged on artistic qualit
 y\, connection to the Nordic region\, relevance to the artistic fields cov
 ered\, how the concept incorporates democratic values such as openness\, t
 rust and artistic freedom\, capacity to implement the collaboration\, publ
 ic appeal for the intended audiences\, public accessibility\, potential fo
 r long-term impact and future collaboration\, and organisational and finan
 cial capacity. Selected projects are publicly announced in May 2027.\n\nOr
 ganisation: Nordic Council of Ministers\nLocation: Projects take place pri
 marily in the applicant organisation's home country\, in European countrie
 s neighbouring the Nordic region: primarily Belgium\, Estonia\, France\, G
 ermany\, Ireland\, Lithuania\, Latvia\, the Netherlands\, Poland and the U
 nited Kingdom.\nAward: EUR 100\,000 to EUR 800\,000 per project. The total
  pool for the initiative is EUR 800\,000\, so between one and eight propos
 als will be funded. Co-financing from local\, national or other sources is
  a prerequisite for eligibility. No application fee.\nDuration: Projects m
 ust take place between 1 June 2027 and 1 June 2028\n\nMore: https://www.no
 rden.org/en/nordiccurrents?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_mediu
 m=referral
URL:https://www.norden.org/en/nordiccurrents?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Projects take place primarily in the applicant organisation's home
  country\, in European countries neighbouring the Nordic region: primarily
  Belgium\, Estonia\, France\, Germany\, Ireland\, Lithuania\, Latvia\, the
  Netherlands\, Poland and the United Kingdom.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:teatri-riflessi-2027-short-performance-competition@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261015
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261016
SUMMARY:Deadline: Teatri Riflessi 2027: Short Performance Competition (12th
  edition)
DESCRIPTION:An open call for at least 10 short performances of maximum 15 m
 inutes for the 12th edition of Teatri Riflessi in Zafferana Etnea\, Sicily
 . THE FORMAT: the competition champions the short performance in its own r
 ight\, not as a reduction of a longer work nor as a sketch for a full-even
 ing adaptation\; its strength is the artistic immediacy and urgency it car
 ries. The framing connects ancient Greek theatre\, conceived as an outdoor
  competition for a large audience\, to the more provocative tendencies of 
 happenings and performance art that lean on the beholder's desire to parti
 cipate. STRUCTURE: semi-finalists perform on the evenings of Thursday 15 a
 nd Friday 16 July 2027\; the Programmers' Panel and the Scholars' & Critic
 s' Panel determine the finalists\, who perform again in the final on Sunda
 y 18 July. The panels bring together around 30 Italian and international a
 rtistic directors\, curators\, festival and theatre programmers\, journali
 sts\, critics and performing arts scholars\, many from the Teatri Riflessi
  Network and Anticorpi XL Network\; the full list is published in June 202
 7. Past partner organisations include Antistatic Festival (Sofia)\, ATER F
 ondazione\, BIG Festival\, Cantieri Danza\, Circuito CLAPS\, COORPI\, Danc
 e ICONS (Washington DC)\, Danza Urbana\, Derida Dance Centre\, EN-KNAP and
  Spanski Borci (Ljubljana)\, Fabbrica Europa\, Festival Ammutinamenti\, Cl
 uny Danse (France)\, Danza Canarias dentro y fuera (Tenerife)\, FIND Festi
 val\, Fondazione Armunia and Festival Inequilibrio\, Fondazione Claudia Lo
 mbardi per il teatro (Lugano)\, Fondazione Sipario Toscana\, Fondazione To
 scana Spettacolo\, Galway Dance (Ireland)\, Interplay Festival (Turin)\, L
 'Arboreto Teatro Dimora\, La Piccionaia\, Movimento Danza\, New Dance For 
 Asia International Festival (Daegu)\, Puglia Culture\, Toplocentrala (Sofi
 a) and Umbria Danza Festival. MONEY: each selected performance receives a 
 gross fee of EUR 500 to EUR 1\,100 depending on cast size\, plus a possibl
 e travel contribution of up to EUR 250. Awards are Best Performance EUR 1\
 ,200 (from the combined weighted scores of both panels\, the Jury of the F
 inal\, the Youth Jury and the Citizens' Jury)\, Best Dramaturgy EUR 500 an
 d the Scholars' and Critics' Award EUR 500 (both from the Scholars' & Crit
 ics' Panel)\, Best Direction EUR 500 and Best Performer EUR 500 (both from
  the Programmers' Panel)\, the 'Valentina Nicosia' Fund of EUR 500 (joint 
 decision of both panels)\, the 'Sandra Bressan' research fund of EUR 1\,80
 0 (awarded by the Citizens' Jury to reinvite a company to Sicily to work w
 ith the community)\, and the Youth Jury Award of EUR 300 to a semi-finalis
 t\, alongside the Audience Special Mention\, the Companies' Choice Award a
 nd the Technical Crew Award. Fees\, reimbursements and prizes are paid aft
 er the festival\, provided all regulations and deadlines are met. FEES TO 
 APPLY: none. Only selected companies pay a EUR 50 deposit on selection\, f
 ully refunded after the festival. RULES: performances should aim for 15 mi
 nutes\, with total stage occupancy including set-up and dismantling capped
  at 20 minutes. Multiple entries are allowed but need a separate form each
 . No major changes are permitted after selection\; minor adjustments\, esp
 ecially cast substitutions\, must be communicated in advance and authorise
 d by IterCulture APS. Works in languages other than Italian are welcome an
 d are presented with Italian and English surtitles prepared by IterCulture
  APS. APPLICATION: a digital form linked in Article 5 of the open call reg
 ulations\, with Annex A for dramaturgy (the complete script where one exis
 ts\, or for dance\, physical theatre and non-text work a short document on
  the dramaturgical research and choreographic structure) and a full video 
 recording of the complete work\; excerpts and teasers are not accepted\, a
 nd for rehearsed readings\, directorial notes on costumes\, lighting and s
 et design are mandatory. COMMITMENT: rehearsal attendance is compulsory\, 
 with the day assigned by the Competition Manager after selection\; compani
 es must arrive in Zafferana Etnea by their rehearsal day (rehearsals run 1
 3 and 14 July 2027) and stay until the festival closes at 23:30 on Sunday 
 18 July. Rehearsals and public events run mainly in afternoons and evening
 s\; the only compulsory morning commitment is the panel feedback session o
 n Sunday 18 July. Online information sessions are held on Google Meet on T
 uesday 22 September 2026\, with the English session at 19:30 CEST. Deadlin
 e 15 October 2026 at 5pm CET.\n\nOrganisation: IterCulture APS\nLocation: 
 Zafferana Etnea\, Sicily\, Italy. Open to Italian and international compan
 ies and artists. Semi-finals 15 and 16 July 2027\, final 18 July 2027\; re
 hearsals 13 and 14 July.\nAward: Gross performance fee of EUR 500 to EUR 1
 \,100 (VAT included) per selected short performance\, scaled by the number
  of performers on stage\, plus a possible travel contribution of up to EUR
  250 subject to available funding\, place of origin\, cast size and absenc
 e of external touring grants. At least 10 performances are selected. Compe
 tition prizes: Best Performance EUR 1\,200\; Best Dramaturgy\, Best Direct
 ion\, Best Performer and the Scholars' and Critics' Award EUR 500 each\; '
 Valentina Nicosia' Fund EUR 500\; 'Sandra Bressan' research fund EUR 1\,80
 0\; Youth Jury Award EUR 300\; plus non-cash awards (Audience Special Ment
 ion\, Companies' Choice\, Technical Crew). Panel members and Teatri Rifles
 si Network partners may also award special prizes in the form of artist re
 sidencies and touring opportunities. Application is free\; selected compan
 ies pay a EUR 50 deposit that is fully refunded after the festival.\nDurat
 ion: Festival participation 13 to 18 July 2027\, with mandatory rehearsals
  and presence until 23:30 on 18 July\n\nMore: https://www.iterculture.eu/e
 n/teatri-riflessi/tr2027/tr12/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.iterculture.eu/en/teatri-riflessi/tr2027/tr12/?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Zafferana Etnea\, Sicily\, Italy. Open to Italian and internationa
 l companies and artists. Semi-finals 15 and 16 July 2027\, final 18 July 2
 027\; rehearsals 13 and 14 July.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iffr-darkroom-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Darkroom 2027: Work-in-Progress Showcase (IFFR Pro)
DESCRIPTION:Darkroom is IFFR's work-in-progress platform\, running during C
 ineMart\, where recently completed or nearly finished films are launched t
 o the international film industry. Selected teams present an excerpt of th
 eir work to international sales agents\, festival programmers\, buyers and
  other industry professionals in a closed presentation\, and receive tailo
 red mentorship matched to the project's needs from senior editors\, market
 ing strategists\, festival experts and distribution professionals. All Dar
 kroom projects get a curated agenda of one-to-one meetings with potential 
 creative and business partners plus facilitated informal networking\, and 
 IFFR Pro awards are handed out to projects from the selection. ELIGIBILITY
 : the project must have been supported by IFFR through at least one of the
  following: new for 2027\, the filmmaker has been selected for the Tiger C
 ompetition or Bright Future programme at IFFR within the last 10 years\; t
 he project has been supported by the Hubert Bals Fund through any of its f
 unding schemes\; the project was formerly presented at BoostNL\; the proje
 ct was formerly presented at CineMart\; or the project's main producer or 
 main co-producer has been a Rotterdam Lab participant. In addition\, the p
 roject must have no sales agent attached\, Darkroom must be its first work
 -in-progress showcase\, and it must not have a confirmed world or internat
 ional premiere at the time of application (if that changes after selection
  at a winter festival\, applicants must tell the Darkroom team immediately
  so eligibility can be rechecked). Projects that do not meet all criteria 
 are ineligible. Recent editions selected 6 to 12 projects. Deadline 1 Octo
 ber 2026\, 22:00 UTC. Free to submit.\n\nOrganisation: International Film 
 Festival Rotterdam (IFFR)\nLocation: Rotterdam\, Netherlands. Presentation
 s take place during CineMart at IFFR 2027 (28 January to 7 February 2027).
  Open internationally: eligibility is defined by the project's prior conne
 ction to IFFR\, not by the applicant's country.\nAward: No participation f
 ee and no fee to submit. Selected projects compete for the IFFR Pro awards
  attached to Darkroom: the Eurimages Outreach Award of EUR 30\,000 (shared
  with Lightroom)\, the HBF Empowerment Award of EUR 10\,000\, the Filmmore
  Work-in-progress Post-production Award of EUR 5\,000\, and the 4DR Studio
 s Award (an in-kind studio or workshop prize\, shared with CineMart). Part
 icipation also includes tailored mentorship\, a closed industry presentati
 on and a curated agenda of one-to-one meetings. Award line-up is per the m
 ost recent published edition and may change for 2027.\nDuration: Work-in-p
 rogress showcase during CineMart at IFFR 2027\, 28 January to 7 February 2
 027\n\nMore: https://iffr.com/en/iffr-pro/darkroom?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://iffr.com/en/iffr-pro/darkroom?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Rotterdam\, Netherlands. Presentations take place during CineMart 
 at IFFR 2027 (28 January to 7 February 2027). Open internationally: eligib
 ility is defined by the project's prior connection to IFFR\, not by the ap
 plicant's country.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mpi-empirical-aesthetics-inhabit-16-17-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: INHABIT #16 and #17: Artist-in-Residence 2027 (Max Planck
  Institute for Empirical Aesthetics)
DESCRIPTION:INHABIT is the artist-in-residence programme of the Max Planck 
 Institute for Empirical Aesthetics in Frankfurt am Main\, which invites ar
 tists from various disciplines to collaborate with its scientists and rese
 archers\, treating artistic thinking and knowledge as reflective practices
  in their own right. The 2027 call is addressed to artists from the perfor
 ming arts and music\, who spend four months creating new work\, or develop
 ing an existing project\, inside a scientific research environment and in 
 dialogue with the institute's researchers. Two slots are open for 2027: IN
 HABIT #16 from April to July 2027 and INHABIT #17 from September to Decemb
 er 2027. Work developed during the residency is presented in cooperation w
 ith local arts institutions as an exhibition\, performance\, concert or ot
 her format\, complemented by discursive formats. WHO THEY ARE LOOKING FOR:
  artists from the performing arts and music interested in interdisciplinar
 y collaboration between artistic and scientific fields\; artists open to d
 eveloping and sharing a project as an exhibition\, performance\, concert o
 r another format\; and artists active in contemporary culture who respond 
 to the institute with innovative and challenging ideas. WHAT IS OFFERED: a
 n artist's fee of EUR 8\,000\, an accommodation fee of EUR 2\,000\, a proj
 ect-dependent production budget\, travel costs to and in Frankfurt\, reali
 sation of the final project\, collaborations with scientists\, access to t
 he library\, sound studio and scientific facilities\, and curatorial suppo
 rt during the residency. APPLY through the institute's e-recruiting portal
  by 1 September 2026\, 23:59 CET\; the jury decision follows in November 2
 026. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Max Planck Institute for Empirical Aesthetic
 s\nLocation: Frankfurt am Main\, Germany\nAward: Artist's fee of EUR 8\,00
 0 plus an accommodation fee of EUR 2\,000\, a production budget depending 
 on the project\, and travel costs to and within Frankfurt. Also included: 
 realisation of the final project\, collaborations with scientists\, access
  to the institute's library\, sound studio and scientific facilities\, and
  curatorial support throughout the residency. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: Four months. Two slots: INHABIT #16 runs April to July 2027\, INHABIT #
 17 runs September to December 2027\n\nMore: https://www.aesthetics.mpg.de/
 en/research/inhabit-artist-in-residence.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.aesthetics.mpg.de/en/research/inhabit-artist-in-residence.h
 tml?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Frankfurt am Main\, Germany
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:platform-dali-residencies-2027-what-is-real@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Platform Dali Open Call for Residencies 2027: What is rea
 l?
DESCRIPTION:Platform Dali launches its first annual Open Call for Residenci
 es as part of its Research and Creation Programme\, with the inaugural 202
 7 edition built around the question 'What is real?'. Artists of any nation
 ality and any discipline are invited to propose a research and creation re
 sidency project developed through close engagement with one of Platform Da
 li's partner scientific institutions: the Barcelona Supercomputing Center 
 (BSC-CNS)\, ICFO (the Institute of Photonic Sciences)\, the Institute of M
 arine Sciences (ICM-CSIC)\, the Institute for High Energy Physics (IFAE)\,
  and the ecosystem of the Barcelona Biomedical Research Park (PRBB)\, incl
 uding the Centre for Genomic Regulation (CRG) and EMBL Barcelona. Five art
 ists are selected\, each hosted by one of the partner institutions. WHAT T
 HE PROGRAMME EXPECTS: research is treated as a creative practice in its ow
 n right\, and residents are explicitly not expected to arrive with a fully
  defined artwork. The residency is designed to support an initial phase of
  open-ended enquiry inside a scientific research environment\, letting que
 stions\, methods and ideas emerge through dialogue and experimentation\; t
 hat research phase then informs the development and production of a new ar
 twork\, so research and creation run as sequential\, interconnected stages
 . TIMELINE: open call launched 2 July 2026\; applications close 7 Septembe
 r 2026 at 23:59 CET\; selected artists notified by November 2026\; residen
 ts publicly announced 2 December 2026. Two residency periods are available
 \, February to April 2027 and September to November 2027\, with final date
 s agreed between Platform Dali\, the host scientific institution and each 
 resident. Full application criteria\, residency structure and terms and co
 nditions are on the open call page.\n\nOrganisation: Platform Dali\nLocati
 on: Barcelona\, Spain\, hosted at one of Platform Dali's partner scientifi
 c institutions. Open to artists of any nationality.\nAward: Artist fee of 
 EUR 7\,500 per selected artist\, plus access to a production budget of up 
 to EUR 25\,000 for the development of a new artwork. Five artists are sele
 cted. For artists based outside Barcelona\, Platform Dali covers travel\, 
 accommodation\, subsistence and travel insurance for both the introductory
  visit and the on-site residency\, within a predefined budget. Also provid
 ed: curatorial support from Platform Dali and the partner institution's te
 am throughout the residency\, and access to the host institution's facilit
 ies\, expertise and research communities\, subject to that institution's r
 egulations and operational requirements. For collectives\, the artist fee\
 , production budget and covered expenses are shared among members\, and a 
 maximum of two members may take part in the introductory visit and on-site
  residency.\nDuration: One of two residency periods: February to April 202
 7\, or September to November 2027. Final dates are agreed between Platform
  Dali\, the host institution and the resident.\n\nMore: https://platformda
 li.org/en/call/open-call-for-residencies-2027-what-is-real/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://platformdali.org/en/call/open-call-for-residencies-2027-what-is
 -real/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Barcelona\, Spain\, hosted at one of Platform Dali's partner scien
 tific institutions. Open to artists of any nationality.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tartu-city-of-literature-residency-autumn-2026@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tartu UNESCO City of Literature Residency for Writers and
  Translators\, Autumn 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Estonian Literary Society\, which coordinates Tartu UNESCO 
 City of Literature\, runs an international residency programme for writers
  and translators that has hosted authors from Slovenia\, Ireland\, Hungary
 \, the USA\, the UK\, Ukraine and Canada since 2017. This is the open call
  for the autumn 2026 slot: one resident stays two months in the Karl Risti
 kivi residency apartment in Tartu\, working on their own writing or transl
 ation while connecting with the local literary scene. WHAT THE RESIDENCY O
 FFERS: a EUR 600 monthly stipend\, travel reimbursement up to EUR 350\, fr
 ee accommodation\, a local support coordinator to help arrange cultural en
 gagements\, and opportunities to perform and present at literary events. T
 he autumn period covers the interdisciplinary literary festival Crazy Tart
 u on 12 to 14 November\, and the resident is offered a slot to perform the
 re alongside writers\, poets\, musicians and artists from Estonia and abro
 ad. ELIGIBILITY: open to writers\, poets and translators of Estonian liter
 ature of any nationality\; applicants need an upper-intermediate level of 
 spoken English\, at least one published book\, screenplay\, theatre script
  or translation from Estonian into another language\, a demonstrable inter
 est in Estonian culture and literature\, a willingness to take part in loc
 al literary activities\, and a commitment to working on their own literary
  project during the stay. HOW TO APPLY: complete the online application fo
 rm linked from the residency page and upload a fragment of up to five page
 s of a published text (in Estonian or English) or of a published translati
 on. SELECTION: made by a committee representing the Estonian Literary Soci
 ety\, the Estonian Writers Union\, the Estonian Literary Museum and the Ta
 rtu Literature House Foundation. Results are announced before 15 September
  2026. The programme's stated aims are to enhance international exchange\,
  diversify the local literary scene\, introduce new foreign authors and th
 eir work to Estonian readers\, and build lasting connections beyond the re
 sidency period. Questions: Marja Unt\, eks@kirjandus.ee. Deadline 1 Septem
 ber 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Estonian Literary Society (Eesti Kirjanduse Sel
 ts)\, Focal Point for Tartu UNESCO City of Literature\nLocation: Tartu\, E
 stonia. Open to writers\, poets and translators of Estonian literature wor
 ldwide.\nAward: Monthly stipend of EUR 600 for the two-month stay (EUR 1\,
 200 in total)\, plus reimbursement of travel costs up to EUR 350. Free acc
 ommodation is provided in the Karl Ristikivi residency apartment\, which h
 as a bedroom\, living room\, kitchen\, bathroom and exhibition room. One r
 esident is selected. No application fee.\nDuration: Two months\, 15 Octobe
 r to 15 December 2026\, with some flexibility on exact dates\n\nMore: http
 s://tartu.kirjandus.ee/residency-autumn-2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://tartu.kirjandus.ee/residency-autumn-2026?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Tartu\, Estonia. Open to writers\, poets and translators of Estoni
 an literature worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:archipel-butor-livre-de-dialogue-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Archipel Butor Livre de dialogue Creation Residency 2027
DESCRIPTION:Archipel Butor runs its creation residency out of the former ho
 me of the writer Michel Butor in Lucinges\, Haute-Savoie\, and this call i
 s for a livre de dialogue\, a dialogue book made jointly by a writer and a
  visual artist. The residency exists to give a pair concentrated time to d
 evelop a book together and to raise the profile of artist book culture and
  of collaboration at the centre of artistic process. WHO APPLIES: a pair m
 ade up of an author with a project for a dialogue book and a visual artist
  collaborating on it. The two do not need to have worked together before\,
  but both must be present during the shared periods. WHAT IS PROVIDED: EUR
  2\,000 gross per month per artist\, up to two return trips per person wit
 hin France and Europe\, and accommodation in the writer's house. STRUCTURE
 : two months in total\, taken as eight weeks which may be split across the
  year from January 2027\, with 70 percent of the time given to creation an
 d 30 percent to mediation and public engagement activities. A videoconfere
 nce is held with the selected laureates beforehand to arrange on-site sche
 duling and the mediation sessions. HOW TO APPLY: follow the official call 
 document downloadable from the residency page\, submitting a single PDF to
  accueil@archipel-butor.fr containing a joint letter\, biographies of both
  practitioners\, the artistic proposal\, technical specifications and the 
 proposed mediation activities. NOTE: the call and application file are in 
 French. Deadline 30 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Archipel Butor\nLocation
 : Lucinges (74380)\, Haute-Savoie\, France\, in the house of the writer Mi
 chel Butor.\nAward: Creation grant of EUR 2\,000 gross per month per artis
 t\, so roughly EUR 4\,000 each across the two months and EUR 8\,000 for th
 e pair. Up to two return journeys per person are covered within France and
  Europe. Accommodation is provided in Michel Butor's writer's house. No ap
 plication fee.\nDuration: Two months in total\, taken as eight weeks that 
 may be split\, starting from January 2027. Time is split 70 percent creati
 on and 30 percent mediation activities.\n\nMore: https://www.archipel-buto
 r.fr/residence-creation/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=
 referral
URL:https://www.archipel-butor.fr/residence-creation/?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Lucinges (74380)\, Haute-Savoie\, France\, in the house of the wri
 ter Michel Butor.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:adagp-sgdl-arcane-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Arcane Grant 2026 for Artist Books
DESCRIPTION:Artists' books get little support\, financially or in distribut
 ion\, despite their place in art history and in the careers of the people 
 who make them. ADAGP\, the French collecting society for copyright in the 
 visual arts\, and SGDL\, the French society of authors\, created the Arcan
 e grant in response: EUR 9\,000 to one winning project for the conception 
 and production of an artist's book made collaboratively by a visual artist
  and a writer. WHO CAN APPLY: a pair consisting of a professional visual a
 rtist from the French scene (working or residing in France for at least fi
 ve years\, or a French national living abroad) and a French-language autho
 r working in fiction\, non-fiction\, poetry\, children's literature or the
 atre who has already been published by a publisher. A trio is also possibl
 e where a publishing structure joins the project. WHAT COUNTS AS AN ARTIST
 'S BOOK: a creation\, a work of art in book form\, in a limited print run 
 of between 1 and 500 copies\, which may be signed or numbered. Explicitly 
 not eligible: magazines\, fanzines\, limited editions of an ordinary book\
 , and any publication that simply presents an artist's work\, such as mono
 graphs or illustrated books. APPLICATION: the file must be in French and i
 ncludes a jointly written project presentation of up to three pages coveri
 ng the artistic and editorial vision\, constituent elements\, methods of r
 ealisation and desired final form\; a one-page technical sheet giving titl
 e\, print run\, format\, page count\, paper quality\, printing techniques 
 and materials\; a provisional timetable\; and a detailed balanced provisio
 nal budget of expenses and financing. Artistic elements are a CV of exhibi
 tions and publications (2 pages) and a portfolio with website link (10 pag
 es) for the artist\, a CV and website link for the author (2 pages)\, and 
 a one-page editorial line for the publishing structure if there is one\; c
 ontact details must be omitted from these. Administrative elements are swo
 rn declarations that the project is a new creation with copyright held sol
 ely by the pair\, that the artist is from the French scene\, and\, if appl
 icable\, that the publishing structure undertakes to publish the work. Tem
 plates are downloadable from the Required Documents page. TIMELINE: call l
 aunched 7 July 2026\, applications close 7 September 2026 at 2pm\, jury me
 ets November 2026\, winner announced end of November 2026\, and the work m
 ust be published within two years of that announcement. The regulations mu
 st be read before applying\, and submitting an application constitutes acc
 eptance of them. Contact: arcane@adagp.fr.\n\nOrganisation: ADAGP and SGDL
  (Societe des Gens de Lettres)\nLocation: France. Open to a professional v
 isual artist from the French scene (working or residing in France for at l
 east five years\, or of French nationality living abroad) paired with a Fr
 ench-language author.\nAward: EUR 9\,000 to the single winning project\, s
 upporting the conception and production of an artist's book. NOTE ON TAX: 
 ADAGP grants count as primary artistic income under the French Intellectua
 l Property Code (art. L112-2)\, so the sum falls under the artist-author s
 ocial security and tax regime\, subject to URSSAF contributions and income
  tax declared as non-commercial profits (micro-BNC or controlled declarati
 on). VAT is not due. No application fee.\nDuration: The winning duo or tri
 o commits to publishing the artist's book within two years of the announce
 ment\n\nMore: https://candidatures.adagp.fr/en/?option=com_emundus&view=pr
 ogramme&cid=187&Itemid=1531&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://candidatures.adagp.fr/en/?option=com_emundus&view=programme&cid
 =187&Itemid=1531&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:France. Open to a professional visual artist from the French scene
  (working or residing in France for at least five years\, or of French nat
 ionality living abroad) paired with a French-language author.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:linz-kultur-arbeitsstipendien-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Linz Kultur Working Grants 2026 (Arbeitsstipendien)
DESCRIPTION:The city of Linz awards project-related working grants to susta
 in and develop its art scene\, in line with the Linz cultural development 
 plan and the city's status as a UNESCO City of Media Arts. The stated aims
  are enabling the further development of artistic practice\, supporting ar
 tistic careers\, and securing a professional artistic existence and innova
 tive production in Linz. Each scholarship is worth EUR 5\,000 over five mo
 nths\, with EUR 30\,000 available in total this year. DISCIPLINES: fine ar
 ts and photography\, literature\, music\, performing arts\, interdisciplin
 ary art forms\, media art and film. WHAT TO SUBMIT: a concrete artistic pr
 oject\, which may be an independent work\, a series of works\, or the cont
 inuation of an earlier series or project. Preparatory work and concept pha
 ses are also eligible. Projects already funded by the City of Linz cannot 
 be submitted\, and only one submission per person per year is permitted. E
 LIGIBILITY: artists of any citizenship who can demonstrate that their cent
 re of work is in Linz\, in the form of a permanent site for their artistic
  work\, and who have a sufficient connection to the city. SELECTION CRITER
 IA: the expert jury assesses sufficient Linz connection\, innovative artis
 tic potential\, independent artistic expression\, a clearly recognisable c
 onception of the artistic intention in the proposal\, and the quality and 
 continuity of the artistic work. REPORTING OBLIGATION: within three months
  of the scholarship ending\, a written progress report of 1\,800 to 5\,400
  characters including spaces must be submitted with supporting documentati
 on\, and after review it is published on the City of Linz website. The pro
 ject need not be finished\, but significant progress must be evident. If n
 o report is submitted\, or the report shows insufficient progress\, the sc
 holarship must be repaid under the City of Linz General Funding Guidelines
 . REQUIRED DOCUMENTS: the completed online application form\, a project de
 scription of up to 10 pages including the innovative approach and planned 
 schedule\, a biography with catalogue of works and any exhibition or prese
 ntation list\, an artistic portfolio\, and references and press reviews. N
 OTE: the online form and the call documents are in German. Contact: lkf.kb
 @mag.linz.at. Deadline 7 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: City of Linz (Li
 nz Kultur\, Arts and Cultural Funding Department)\nLocation: Linz\, Austri
 a. Open to artists of any citizenship whose centre of work is in Linz.\nAw
 ard: EUR 5\,000 per scholarship over a five-month period\, from a total po
 t of EUR 30\,000 this year\, so approximately six grants. No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Five months\n\nMore: https://www.linz.at/serviceguide/viewc
 hapter.php?chapter_id=123406&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
URL:https://www.linz.at/serviceguide/viewchapter.php?chapter_id=123406&utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Linz\, Austria. Open to artists of any citizenship whose centre of
  work is in Linz.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:les-antipodes-numeric-2026-the-word@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: Numeric 2026: The Word (Les antipodes Research and Creati
 on Residency)
DESCRIPTION:Two research and creation residency grants in Agullana\, Catalo
 nia\, for artists\, thinkers and scientists of any branch (formal\, natura
 l\, social\, applied) working at the intersection of art\, culture\, scien
 ce and thought. THIS YEAR'S THEME\, The Word\, is squarely about large lan
 guage models. The framework moves from the word as creative principle acro
 ss traditions (the Judeo-Christian creating God\, the Quran as uncreated w
 ord\, the Sanskrit Om and the Vedas\, mantras that summon rather than desc
 ribe) to science relocating that intuition in the cell (Sagan's four-lette
 r alphabet of DNA as a material text\, Margulis countering that the langua
 ge of life is chemistry\, Witzany holding that all cell interactions are s
 ign-mediated)\, and then to LLMs\, which for the first time detach the wor
 d from the flesh and generate text through a probabilistic architecture tr
 ained on human language. The call asks what LLMs reveal about authorship\,
  originality and meaning when a system generates text that moves\, persuad
 es or deceives without experience or intention\, and whether we can still 
 say the models operate exclusively on human experience once they ingest th
 eir own output. It explicitly wants work that blurs comprehension and simu
 lation\, authorship and recombination\, and it names antecedents worth kno
 wing before applying: Ramon Llull's Ars Magna\, Joan Brossa's Sextina cibe
 rnetica\, Oulipo constraint writing\, oracles and divination\, oral-formul
 aic transmission\, translation as probabilistic approximation\, exquisite 
 corpses\, Burroughs's cut-ups\, automatic writing\, musical improvisation 
 and scientific simulation. Les antipodes asks for engagement 'beyond the i
 nstrumental use of the tools' and away from utopian and dystopian framings
 . WHAT YOU GET: spacious\, luminous\, independent work spaces plus a share
 d terrace in the centre of the village\, spaces for meeting\, dialogue and
  public presentation\, introductions to local\, academic\, artistic and pr
 ofessional contacts on request\, and support organising any activities tha
 t arise. The residency ends with an informal presentation\, and results ma
 y feed into the publications or public programme of Les antipodes. IMPORTA
 NTLY\, this is not an acquisition prize and involves no transfer of rights
 : authorship and intellectual property stay with the creators\, and select
 ion implies no acquisition of the work. Credits on resulting work must ack
 nowledge Numeric 2026 and its collaborators. WHO CAN APPLY: open to everyo
 ne of legal age\, any discipline or origin\, individually or as a collecti
 ve\, with the stipend shared among collective members. Applications must b
 e in Catalan or English whatever the working language of the project\, sub
 mitted through the online form with an optional PDF of up to 5 pages and 1
 0 MB. One application per person or collective. EVALUATION\, in order of w
 eight: singularity and innovation\; relevance to this year's LLM framework
 \; and interaction and transfer of value\, meaning relations with the soci
 al and geographical environment of Agullana and the Emporda and a plan for
  presenting the outcome in editorial\, on-site\, performative\, sound\, au
 diovisual or other form. The call may be declared void if no proposal reac
 hes the required level. PRACTICAL: residents must hold their own health co
 ver (EHIC for EU citizens\, or valid travel or health insurance for Spain 
 otherwise) and are responsible for their own equipment. Supported by the G
 eneralitat de Catalunya\, Agullana and Figueres town councils\, and Accion
  Cultural Espanola through the PICE national residencies programme. Terms 
 are published in Arabic\, Catalan\, English\, Spanish\, French and Chinese
 .\n\nOrganisation: Les antipodes\, with Bolit Centre d'Art Contemporani Gi
 rona and Museu de l'Emporda Figueres\nLocation: Agullana\, Alt Emporda\, C
 atalonia\, Spain. A village of 900 people\, 20 km from Figueres and the Vi
 lafant high-speed rail station\, 40 km from Perpignan\, 60 km from Girona\
 , 160 km from Barcelona.\nAward: Two grants. Each provides a stipend of EU
 R 1\,000 as taxable base (taxes and withholdings apply)\, round-trip trave
 l within the European Union\, and accommodation and meals for the four wee
 ks in Agullana. Travel and meals cover the resident only\, though the shar
 ed apartment can host immediate family by arrangement. Numeric also provid
 es the resources specified in the application and agreed during preparatio
 n. No application fee. Note that if a selected candidate lacks the profess
 ional registration needed to receive the stipend and payment has to be arr
 anged through Les antipodes\, the resulting costs are deducted from the st
 ipend.\nDuration: Four weeks\, 2 to 30 November 2026\, with both residenci
 es running simultaneously. Preceded by one month of preparation and follow
 ed by a two-month closing period. Applications 23 July to 8 September 2026
 \; decision announced 22 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://lesantipodes.com
 /numeric/2026_bases_en.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://lesantipodes.com/numeric/2026_bases_en.html?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Agullana\, Alt Emporda\, Catalonia\, Spain. A village of 900 peopl
 e\, 20 km from Figueres and the Vilafant high-speed rail station\, 40 km f
 rom Perpignan\, 60 km from Girona\, 160 km from Barcelona.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cultural-horizons-residencies-subgrants-2027@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cultural Horizons Residencies: Subgrants for EU-Ukrainian
  Residency Projects 2027
DESCRIPTION:Cultural Horizons strengthens the cultural sector in Ukraine by
  promoting joint EU-Ukrainian cultural initiatives and expanding access fo
 r Ukrainians affected by the war to cultural and artistic networks in Euro
 pe. Running 2026 to 2027\, it provides subgrants for projects developed th
 rough EU-Ukrainian partnerships and builds a Europe-wide network of cultur
 al expert mentors. THIS CALL\, the Residencies strand\, gives subgrants to
  EU-based institutions to run (a) residency placements and/or (b) educatio
 n projects organised on the basis of residencies\, for and with Ukrainian 
 artistic and cultural professionals during 2027. WHO CAN APPLY: artistic a
 nd cultural organisations and initiatives registered within the EU. Consor
 tium applications are allowed but must designate one lead organisation to 
 submit and manage the funding. This is an organisational call\, so individ
 ual Ukrainian artists cannot apply directly\; they participate as the host
 ed residents. Applicants must show they can host Ukrainian professionals i
 n 2027 and complete the project in the stated window. NGOs and public sect
 or institutions working with displaced and vulnerable groups are prioritis
 ed. CONDITIONS: applicants must confirm the absence of ties with the Russi
 an Federation\, meaning no working partnerships with Russia or Russian org
 anisations and no visits to Russian-occupied territories after 2014\, comm
 it to refrain from the glorification of Russian imperialism\, and comply w
 ith the EU sanctions regime. They must also demonstrate a commitment to di
 versity\, equity and inclusion and provide a working environment based on 
 mutual trust\, awareness and intercultural sensitivity. WHO THE MONEY IS M
 EANT TO REACH: Ukrainian artists and cultural practitioners\, whether inde
 pendent or employed\, including early-career specialists\, debut artists\,
  cultural managers\, researchers\, analysts and conservators\; vulnerable 
 groups from Ukraine including internally displaced persons\, young artists
 \, and communities in frontline and conflict-affected areas\; and cultural
  organisations in Ukraine\, with particular focus on small communities\, l
 ocal initiatives\, independent cultural spaces and NGOs. APPLYING: submiss
 ion is through a JotForm hosted by Cultural Vistas and must be complete\, 
 including uploads\, or it will not be processed. You should receive an ema
 il confirmation\; if it does not arrive\, the submission was not received.
  Enquiries to culturalhorizons@culturalvistas.eu. NOTE ON DATES: the eligi
 bility section says projects must be completed within January to August 20
 27 while the application instructions give the implementation phase as 1 J
 anuary to 30 July 2027\, so plan to the earlier end date. Programme backgr
 ound is at culturalvistas.eu under Our Programs\, Professional Study Tours
 \, Cultural Horizons.\n\nOrganisation: Ukrainian Cultural Foundation and C
 ultural Vistas\, funded by the Creative Europe programme of the European U
 nion\nLocation: Applicant organisations must be registered in the EU. Resi
 dencies are hosted in the EU for Ukrainian artistic and cultural professio
 nals.\nAward: EUR 5\,000 to 20\,000 per subgrant\, with a minimum of 20 su
 bgrants to be awarded. No application fee.\nDuration: Projects run between
  1 January and 30 July 2027\, minimum 3 months and maximum 8 months. Appli
 cations 29 June to 30 September 2026\; evaluation October to November 2026
 . Final reports are due within one month of project completion and no late
 r than 15 August 2027.\n\nMore: https://culturalvistas.jotform.com/2617534
 20662859?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culturalvistas.jotform.com/261753420662859?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicant organisations must be registered in the EU. Residencies 
 are hosted in the EU for Ukrainian artistic and cultural professionals.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:le-garage-moderne-oasis-residencies-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Le Garage Moderne: OASIS International Artist Residencies
  2026 (Bordeaux)
DESCRIPTION:Second year of Le Garage Moderne's residency cycle within the E
 uropean OASIS project\, offering five grants to international artists for 
 two-week stays in Bordeaux. THE SITE IS THE POINT. Le Garage Moderne is a 
 working non-profit car and bicycle workshop with a neighbourhood canteen a
 nd a versatile venue for performing arts\, concerts and community gatherin
 gs\, all at once a productive space\, a public space and a community-drive
 n common. Having just moved back into its 19th-century building after a lo
 ng renovation\, the team describes machines and workbenches back in place 
 and a brand-new playground\, and invites residents to take over the space 
 and explore its physical and social possibilities. They ask for SITE-SPECI
 FIC PROPOSALS that play with the verticality\, the scale of the space\, th
 e community and what they call the hive-like vibe of the venue\, where eve
 ryone runs around\, shouts\, gets their hands dirty and learns in the proc
 ess. DISCIPLINES: any\, explicitly including design\, visual art\, perform
 ing arts\, film\, small-scale architecture\, emergency architecture\, craf
 t-making\, street furniture\, sound\, and interdisciplinary or community-b
 ased practices. Proposals combining artistic creation with the spatial and
  the social are especially encouraged\, and the organisers say plainly tha
 t they want to be surprised. WHAT IS EXPECTED: openness to exchange and co
 llaboration with the 11 permanent residents and with 3 local artists recru
 ited through a separate OASIS call\; participation in 2 days of masterclas
 s open to members and community\, where you describe and share your practi
 ce\, technique and working context with fellow OASIS residents\, local art
 ists\, members and staff\; and a public outcome\, which need not be a larg
 e event but must be something the public can experience. RIGHTS: work prod
 uced during the residency remains the artist's property\, but documentatio
 n of it is made by Le Garage Moderne and will be publicised by them for th
 ree years after the residency. Note this is a different OASIS partner call
  from the OASis In the Park (Naples) and Disappearing Landscape (High Tatr
 as) rounds under the same European project.\n\nOrganisation: Le Garage Mod
 erne (European OASIS project)\nLocation: Bacalan district\, Bordeaux\, Fra
 nce. Le Garage Moderne is a non-profit car garage and bicycle workshop\, a
  canteen serving sponsored meals\, and a cultural venue housing 11 permane
 nt artists\, makers and craftswomen\, recently returned to its 19th-centur
 y building after a two-and-a-half-year renovation.\nAward: Five grants. Ea
 ch artist receives a fee of EUR 1\,300 gross (taxes may apply depending on
  legal status)\, up to EUR 600 for production expenses such as raw materia
 ls\, technical services or special tools\, a lump-sum travel grant scaled 
 to distance with top-ups for green travel options\, a per diem of EUR 45 p
 er day\, and accommodation in a self-catering hotel. Access to the shared 
 tools and spaces of Le Garage Moderne is included\, subject to the associa
 tion's regulations. No application fee.\nDuration: Two weeks on site. Five
  overlapping two-week slots\, and applicants may choose one or more: 9 to 
 22 November\, 16 to 29 November\, 23 November to 6 December\, 30 November 
 to 13 December\, and 7 to 20 December 2026. Deadline 1 September 2026\; pr
 e-selection interviews 15 September\; final selection announced 1 October 
 2026.\n\nMore: https://legaragemoderne.org/oasis2026?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://legaragemoderne.org/oasis2026?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Bacalan district\, Bordeaux\, France. Le Garage Moderne is a non-p
 rofit car garage and bicycle workshop\, a canteen serving sponsored meals\
 , and a cultural venue housing 11 permanent artists\, makers and craftswom
 en\, recently returned to its 19th-century building after a two-and-a-half
 -year renovation.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kultur-ensemble-palermo-residencies-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kultur Ensemble Palermo Residencies 2027 (Franco-German D
 uos)
DESCRIPTION:Kultur Ensemble Palermo\, the joint Franco-German cultural inst
 itute uniting the Institut francais Palermo and the Goethe-Institut Palerm
 o since 2021\, offers two three-month residencies promoting research and c
 reation on Franco-German projects with a close link to Palermo and Sicily.
  Residents work from the Bottega\, the 115 sq m artist's studio inaugurate
 d in October 2024 within the Cantieri Culturali alla Zisa. WHO CAN APPLY\,
  and this is the unusual constraint: applications must come from a FRANCO-
 GERMAN DUO\, meaning French or German nationality or working in France or 
 Germany\, and all applicants must be UNDER 36 at the time of the residency
 \, a requirement of the Franco-German Youth Office which funds the scheme.
  Beyond that\, applicants need a practice or project that genuinely justif
 ies the need for a studio\, and a project related to Palermo\, Sicily or t
 he Mediterranean region. COMMITMENTS: participate in at least two educatio
 nal activities such as a presentation of work or a workshop with Palermo s
 chools\; provide communication material for the institutes' social media a
 nd take part in a short video interview\; be free from other professional 
 commitments during the residency\; and hold civil liability insurance and 
 health insurance valid abroad for the full period. A public presentation e
 vent is NOT mandatory and can be discussed with the institutes during the 
 stay. The institutes also help establish contacts with the local cultural 
 network and institutions. APPLYING: all documents in a single PDF\, submit
 ted in duplicate\, one version in French or German and one in English\, to
  kulturensemblepalermo@gmail.com. Required: CV of maximum 2 pages per pers
 on for everyone involved including contact details and date of birth\; a p
 roject description with justification for the studio\, maximum 1 page\; an
 d a portfolio of maximum 10 pages total. An Italian-French-German jury of 
 experts decides at the end of September 2026\, with results announced by e
 mail and published on Kultur Ensemble Palermo's Instagram and the social c
 hannels of both institutes. NOTE ON DATES: the official English call PDF s
 ays 'two three-month residencies in 2026'\, which is an error carried into
  several republished versions. The residency periods listed in the same do
 cument\, and the French institute's own page\, both give 2027. Enquiries: 
 bianca.bozzeda@goethe.de (Goethe-Institut) or judith.testault@institutfran
 cais.it (Institut francais).\n\nOrganisation: Kultur Ensemble Palermo (Ins
 titut francais Palermo and Goethe-Institut Palermo)\, with the Franco-Germ
 an Youth Office (OFAJ/DFJW)\nLocation: Palermo\, Sicily. Studio at the Bot
 tega\, a 115 sq m space within the Cantieri Culturali alla Zisa\, shared w
 ith other residents.\nAward: Two residencies. Each provides a residency gr
 ant of EUR 1\,500 per person per month\, one return journey between home a
 nd Palermo capped at EUR 500 per person\, a budget of up to EUR 500 for re
 search\, creation and production costs (agreed in advance with the institu
 tes)\, and accommodation in the city with private rooms and shared common 
 spaces. Over three months that is EUR 4\,500 per person in grant alone\, a
 nd duos are funded per person. No application fee.\nDuration: Three months
 . Two slots in 2027: 11 January to 4 April\, and 20 September to 12 Decemb
 er. Deadline 6 September 2026 at midnight\; jury decision end of September
  2026.\n\nMore: https://www.institutfrancais.it/fr/palerme/bando-residenze
 -il-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.institutfrancais.it/fr/palerme/bando-residenze-il-2027?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Palermo\, Sicily. Studio at the Bottega\, a 115 sq m space within 
 the Cantieri Culturali alla Zisa\, shared with other residents.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:granada-unesco-writers-in-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: International Granada Writers in Residence Programme 2026
DESCRIPTION:A 30-night residency in Granada for two writers\, run by Granad
 a UNESCO City of Literature with the University of Granada. OPEN TO WRITER
 S RESIDING IN ANY COUNTRY AND WRITING IN ANY LANGUAGE\, which makes this o
 ne of the more genuinely international literary residencies. The programme
  exists to build connections between writers from Granada and writers worl
 dwide\, support the internationalisation of writers associated with Granad
 a\, strengthen the international links of the city's literary ecosystem\, 
 and promote Granada as a welcoming destination for creative talent. ELIGIB
 ILITY: applicants must have published AT LEAST TWO INDIVIDUALLY AUTHORED B
 OOKS. Contributions to anthologies or collective volumes do not count\, se
 lf-published works are not eligible\, and chapbooks (plaquettes) are not c
 ounted as books. Eligible genres are novels\, essays\, poetry\, drama and 
 hybrid literary forms. An intermediate to advanced level of English and/or
  Spanish is required\, along with a synopsis of the literary project to be
  developed during the residency. TREATED AS AN ASSET BUT NOT MANDATORY: kn
 owledge of Spanish at any level\, an interest in the cultural and historic
 al heritage of Granada\, an extract from a published book translated into 
 Spanish or English running 1\,500 to 3\,000 words\, and one or more letter
 s of recommendation. OBLIGATIONS: residents devote the period to their lit
 erary project\, take part in the public activities and community engagemen
 t the programme organises\, and publicly acknowledge the stay. They may no
 t undertake public activities with other institutions or in other cities w
 ithout prior notification and authorisation from the organisers. APPLYING:
  complete the application form\, a PDF that must be opened in Adobe Acroba
 t\, and email it to talleres@granadaciudaddeliteratura.com. Incomplete for
 ms are treated as invalid. A committee of experts appointed jointly by Gra
 nada UNESCO City of Literature and the University of Granada decides. Past
  residents give a sense of the level: Kirsty Logan and Petra Zist (2017)\,
  Sahar Delijani and Aleksandra Lipczak (2018)\, Luciana Jazmin Coronado an
 d Marli Roode (2019)\, Paulina Flores and Iosi Havilio (2021)\, Taimi Dieg
 uez and Frank Westerman (2022)\, Nell Leyshon and Yuliana Ortiz Ruano (202
 3)\, Karina Sainz Borgo and James Womack (2024)\, Ahlam Bsharat and Nikki 
 Dekker (2025).\n\nOrganisation: Granada UNESCO City of Literature (Arts De
 partment\, Granada City Council) with the University of Granada\nLocation:
  Granada\, Spain. Residency at the Corrala de Santiago\, the University of
  Granada's guest residence.\nAward: Two residencies. Each covers 30 nights
  of accommodation in a single room on a full-board basis at the Corrala de
  Santiago\, plus round-trip travel from the airport nearest the writer's h
 ome to Granada Airport. There is no cash stipend. Transfers between home a
 nd the departure airport\, travel insurance and health insurance are the w
 riter's own responsibility and cost. No application fee.\nDuration: 30 nig
 hts\, Monday 2 November to Tuesday 1 December 2026 inclusive. Deadline 6 S
 eptember 2026\; selected writers announced Thursday 1 October 2026.\n\nMor
 e: https://granadaciudaddeliteratura.com/en/international-granada-writers-
 in-residence-programme-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
URL:https://granadaciudaddeliteratura.com/en/international-granada-writers-
 in-residence-programme-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Granada\, Spain. Residency at the Corrala de Santiago\, the Univer
 sity of Granada's guest residence.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ecf-academic-research-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: European Cultural Foundation: Academic Research Fellowshi
 p (ECF Historical Archives)
DESCRIPTION:The academic track of the ECF Fellowship Programme\, which fost
 ers new research engaging with the history of the European Cultural Founda
 tion and the broader role of culture in European integration. The ECF was 
 established in 1954 by figures including Denis de Rougemont\, Robert Schum
 an\, Hendrik Brugmans and Prince Bernhard of the Netherlands\, on the beli
 ef that Europe will not be built by treaties alone. Over seventy years it 
 has produced programmes\, institutes\, campaigns and collaborations\, incl
 uding a contribution to shaping and implementing the ERASMUS student mobil
 ity scheme. That legacy is now preserved at the Historical Archives of the
  European Union in Florence alongside the records of the EU's main institu
 tions\, a placement that itself recognises the Foundation's role in Europe
 an integration. ELIGIBILITY for the Academic Research Fellowship: research
 ers in European studies\, cultural studies\, history and related fields ac
 ross the humanities and social sciences\, specifically PhD and postdoctora
 l researchers\, early-career researchers within ten years of completing a 
 master's or doctoral degree\, and independent scholars with an active rese
 arch profile. Fellows work at the HAEU embedded in the international schol
 arly community of the European University Institute\, with the archives ho
 used at Villa Salviati. Applications and all supporting documentation must
  be submitted in English through the online form. NOTE: this is the EUR 5\
 ,000 academic track. The EUR 10\,000 Artistic Research Fellowship\, open t
 o professional creatives in visual arts\, performing arts\, film and audio
 visual practices\, podcasting and audio storytelling\, documentary\, journ
 alism and literary practices\, is listed separately on the desk and shares
  the same deadline.\n\nOrganisation: European Cultural Foundation\, hosted
  at the Historical Archives of the European Union\nLocation: Historical Ar
 chives of the European Union\, Villa Salviati\, Florence\, Italy\, within 
 the European University Institute.\nAward: EUR 5\,000 grant to support a r
 esearch stay at the HAEU\, covering travel\, accommodation and living expe
 nses. The parallel Artistic Research Fellowship carries EUR 10\,000 and ad
 ditionally covers artistic production. No application fee.\nDuration: Rese
 arch stay at the HAEU. Outcomes must be completed and publicly presented o
 r published within two years of signing the Grant Agreement. Deadline 30 S
 eptember 2026 at 17:00 CET.\n\nMore: https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-ca
 lls/new-fellowship-programme-to-research-the-ecf-historical-archives/?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culturalfoundation.eu/open-calls/new-fellowship-programme-to-re
 search-the-ecf-historical-archives/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Historical Archives of the European Union\, Villa Salviati\, Flore
 nce\, Italy\, within the European University Institute.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:unidee-cittadellarte-ecosystems-living-communities-vol3@artificialnouve
 au.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ecosystems as Living Communities vol. III: UniCredit Grou
 p UNIDEE Connective Residencies 2026-27
DESCRIPTION:Third edition of Ecosystems as Living Communities\, supporting 
 two production residencies for emerging international artists working with
  socially engaged practices that address contemporary ecological issues. T
 WO PLACES\, and the split matters: one Italian artist and one internationa
 l artist residing in Europe. The framing follows the mission of Fondazione
  Pistoletto Cittadellarte\, the vision and work of Michelangelo Pistoletto
 \, and the UN Sustainable Development Goals\, and promotes interdisciplina
 ry collaboration across scientific\, technological and humanistic fields. 
 WHAT THEY WANT: projects that investigate ecosystems as interconnected com
 munities of humans and non-humans\, challenging anthropocentric perspectiv
 es and promoting an ecocentric mindset\, and that foster positive and rege
 nerative visions of desirable futures rather than only diagnosing crisis. 
 HOW IT RUNS: the two artists share a research phase including meetings wit
 h tutors\, researchers and experts as well as field visits\, so a collabor
 ative attitude and flexibility as the research evolves are described as es
 sential. Resulting works are publicly presented at Cittadellarte in Biella
  and disseminated online. IMPORTANT ON RIGHTS: the final artwork produced 
 during the residency BECOMES PART OF THE UNICREDIT ART COLLECTION\, so thi
 s is a production residency that ends in acquisition by a corporate collec
 tion\, not a no-strings creation grant. Weigh that against the fee before 
 applying. For large installations\, artists are also required to develop a
  simplified version of the work that can be installed in smaller exhibitio
 n spaces while preserving its conceptual integrity. APPLYING: complete the
  online form and submit the required documents\, all in English\, includin
 g a portfolio and CV as one PDF up to 5 MB containing a selected portfolio
  of maximum 5 projects or artworks most fitting to the call topic\, plus a
  brief biography and CV.\n\nOrganisation: Fondazione Pistoletto Cittadella
 rte and UNIDEE Residency Programs\, in collaboration with UniCredit Group\
 nLocation: Cittadellarte\, Biella\, Italy.\nAward: Two residencies. Each p
 rovides a gross artist fee of EUR 6\,500\, a production budget of up to EU
 R 2\,500 for costs directly related to producing the final artwork (instal
 lation supports\, equipment rental\, materials)\, round-trip travel and re
 search trips with a combined budget of EUR 1\,000 to 1\,500 per artist dep
 ending on distance\, accommodation in a single room with shared facilities
  at Cittadellarte plus a per diem of EUR 25 per day for up to 60 days\, an
 d a research budget of up to EUR 500 for fees to experts contributing to t
 he research. Cittadellarte separately covers technical support for the exh
 ibition installation\, project communication\, and a publication accompany
 ing the residency and final exhibition. No application fee.\nDuration: Res
 idencies take place between December 2026 and April 2027. The structure an
 d schedule are tailored to each project and defined collaboratively with t
 he artists. Deadline 6 September 2026 at 23:59\; shortlisted candidates ma
 y be interviewed online between late September and mid-October\; selection
  by mid-October 2026.\n\nMore: https://unidee.cittadellarte.it/activity.ht
 ml?id=263&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://unidee.cittadellarte.it/activity.html?id=263&utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cittadellarte\, Biella\, Italy.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:unidee-cittadellarte-visiting-research-curator-2027-28@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260824T212444Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: UNIDEE Residency Programs: Visiting Research Curator 2027
 -28
DESCRIPTION:Cittadellarte is recruiting a Visiting Research Curator to shap
 e the research arc of the UNIDEE Residency Modules for 2027-2028\, the rol
 e most recently held by Nina Fiocco and Gaia Martino. Founded by Michelang
 elo Pistoletto in 1999\, the residency has hosted thousands of artists and
  cultural professionals from 52 countries. WHAT THEY WANT: rather than a f
 ixed theme set in advance\, they want a curator who brings their own line 
 of inquiry and develops it collectively through the modules. Suggested the
 matic areas are the evolving landscape of socially engaged art practices\,
  research-driven and process-oriented practice\, and the conditions of cul
 tural work and cultural workers\, aligned with UNIDEE's focus on artistic 
 research in response to ecological\, geopolitical and technological challe
 nges. They also flag a specific interest in how contemporary art practices
  are archived\, drawing on the UNIDEE archive\, which documents over two d
 ecades of socially engaged art initiatives. THIS IS A JOB\, NOT A GRANT: t
 he duties list is long and operational. It covers proposing the overarchin
 g curatorial theme\, designing and delivering a minimum of 8 weekly reside
 ntial modules per year\, writing the curatorial statement and resident ope
 n call\, recruiting mentors and guests\, selecting residents\, hosting and
  facilitating every module in person\, communications and website texts\, 
 a dissemination strategy\, and conceiving and editing publications. There 
 is also teaching-adjacent work with the Unidee Academy of Contemporary Art
 s\, including public lectures and studio visits. Budget the time before ap
 plying. PROFILE: experience curating art in the public sphere and socially
  engaged art\, specialised knowledge of international contemporary art\, e
 xperience designing experimental and collaborative educational activities\
 , and experience with artistic research and dissemination standards. Excel
 lent English required\; Italian welcome but not essential. APPLYING: onlin
 e form with a project proposal of maximum 5000 characters stating your vis
 ion for the modules programme and a development strategy including possibl
 e mentors and guests\, plus a CV\, a portfolio of maximum three relevant p
 rojects\, and TWO LETTERS OF RECOMMENDATION\, so line up your referees ear
 ly. A jury of the UNIDEE Advisory Board\, the Director of Cittadellarte an
 d invited experts decides. They do not give feedback on unsuccessful propo
 sals.\n\nOrganisation: Fondazione Pistoletto Cittadellarte and UNIDEE Resi
 dency Programs\nLocation: Cittadellarte\, Biella\, Italy. Requires in-pers
 on presence at Cittadellarte for a minimum of one month plus the full leng
 th of each module.\nAward: Compensation and contractual terms commensurate
  with the candidate's level of professional experience\, starting from a g
 ross amount of EUR 22\,000 for the appointment\, excluding travel and hosp
 itality\, which are covered separately. No application fee.\nDuration: Tem
 porary appointment from November 2026 to December 2028\, covering the 2027
 -2028 research arc and a minimum of 8 residency modules per year. Deadline
  13 September 2026 at 23:59 CEST\; shortlisted candidates interviewed onli
 ne by mid-October 2026\, with the selected candidate announced a few days 
 later.\n\nMore: https://unidee.cittadellarte.it/activity.html?id=269&utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://unidee.cittadellarte.it/activity.html?id=269&utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cittadellarte\, Biella\, Italy. Requires in-person presence at Cit
 tadellarte for a minimum of one month plus the full length of each module.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
